{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if any two numbers in the given list are closer to each other\n    than the specified threshold.\n\n    The function first handles edge cases for list length.\n    It then sorts the list of numbers and iterates through the sorted list,\n    comparing only adjacent elements. This approach optimizes the time complexity\n    from O(N^2) to O(N log N) due to the sorting step.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of floating-point numbers.\n        threshold: A positive floating-point value defining 'closeness'.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if any two distinct numbers have an absolute difference less than\n        the threshold, False otherwise.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.0, 3.0], 0.5)\n        False\n        >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.8, 3.0, 4.0, 5.0, 2.0], 0.3)\n        True\n        >>> has_close_elements([], 0.5)\n        False\n        >>> has_close_elements([5.0], 0.1)\n        False\n        >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 1.00001], 0.001)\n        True\n        >>> has_close_elements([1.0, 2.0, 1.5], 0.6)\n        True # |1.0 - 1.5| = 0.5 < 0.6\n    \"\"\"\n    # F.6: Handle edge cases for list length\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # Q.S.1, Q.S.2: Optimize performance by sorting the list.\n    # If two numbers are closer than a threshold, they must be adjacent\n    # in a sorted list. This yields O(N log N) complexity (due to sort)\n    # and O(N) space complexity (for sorted_numbers copy).\n    sorted_numbers = sorted(numbers)\n\n    # F.2, F.3, F.4, F.5: Iterate through adjacent elements in the sorted list\n    for i in range(len(sorted_numbers) - 1):\n        diff = abs(sorted_numbers[i+1] - sorted_numbers[i])\n        if diff < threshold:\n            return True # Found a pair closer than threshold\n\n    # If no such pair was found after checking all adjacent elements\n    return False"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/0", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef has_close_elements(numbers: List[float], threshold: float) -> bool:\n    # 1. Check len(numbers) < 2. If true, return False.\n    if len(numbers) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    # 2. Sort numbers in ascending order.\n    numbers.sort()\n\n    # 3. Loop from i = 0 to len(numbers) - 2 (inclusive):\n    for i in range(len(numbers) - 1):\n        # a. Calculate diff = abs(numbers[i+1] - numbers[i]).\n        diff = abs(numbers[i+1] - numbers[i])\n        # b. If diff < threshold, return True.\n        if diff < threshold:\n            return True\n\n    # 4. If the loop finishes, return False.\n    return False"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/1", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses an input string to identify and separate distinct, balanced groups\n    of parentheses.\n\n    The function adheres to the following requirements:\n    - Ignores all space characters in the input string (F.1).\n    - Identifies sequences of parentheses that are balanced (F.2).\n    - Ensures that identified groups are distinct and not nested within\n      each other at the top level (F.3). For example, in `( () )`,\n      `( () )` is considered one group. In `() (())`, these are two\n      non-nested groups.\n    - Extracts each identified group as a separate string (F.4).\\n    - Returns a list containing all extracted group strings (F.5).\n    - If the input string is empty or contains only spaces, an empty list\n      is returned (F.6).\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: The input string, expected to contain '(', ')', and\n                      whitespace characters.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of strings, where each string is a distinct, balanced, and\n        non-nested parenthesis group found in the input.\n    \"\"\"\n    # F.1 Ignore Spaces: Remove all space characters from the input string.\n    cleaned_s = paren_string.replace(\" \", \"\")\n\n    # F.6 Empty Input Handling: If the string is empty after removing spaces,\n    # return an empty list as there are no groups.\n    if not cleaned_s:\n        return []\n\n    groups: List[str] = []\n    balance: int = 0\n    # current_group_start marks the beginning index of the current potential\n    # top-level parenthesis group being tracked.\n    current_group_start: int = 0\n\n    # Iterate through the cleaned string character by character.\n    for i, char in enumerate(cleaned_s):\n        if char == '(':\n            balance += 1\n        elif char == ')':\n            balance -= 1\n\n        # F.2 Identify Balanced Groups & F.3 Ensure Non-Nested Top-Level Groups:\n        # A balanced, non-nested top-level group is completed when the balance\n        # counter returns to 0. This signifies that all parentheses from\n        # `current_group_start` up to the current index `i` are matched.\n        if balance == 0:\n            # F.4 Extract Groups: Extract the substring corresponding to the\n            # identified balanced group.\n            group = cleaned_s[current_group_start : i + 1]\n            groups.append(group)\n            # After extracting a group, update `current_group_start` to the\n            # position immediately after the current group, ready to find\n            # the next potential top-level group.\n            current_group_start = i + 1\n        \n        # If balance becomes negative at any point, it means an unmatched closing\n        # parenthesis appeared. As per the definition of \"Balanced Parentheses\"\n        # in the SRS, such sequences do not form properly balanced groups.\n        # The algorithm implicitly handles this by only adding groups when\n        # `balance` correctly returns to 0, ensuring only valid groups are extracted.\n        # Any remaining unbalanced parts at the end of the string are ignored.\n\n    # F.5 Return List of Groups: Return the list of all collected groups.\n    return groups"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/1", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef separate_paren_groups(paren_string: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Separates a string containing multiple groups of nested parentheses into a list of\n    individual balanced, top-level groups. Ignores spaces in the input string.\n    \"\"\"\n    result: List[str] = []\n    current_group_start: int = 0\n    balance: int = 0\n\n    cleaned_string: str = paren_string.replace(\" \", \"\")\n\n    for i in range(len(cleaned_string)):\n        if cleaned_string[i] == '(':\n            balance += 1\n        elif cleaned_string[i] == ')':\n            balance -= 1\n\n        if balance == 0:\n            # This indicates the end of a complete, balanced, top-level group.\n            # Extract the substring from cleaned_string starting at current_group_start\n            # up to i + 1 (inclusive of cleaned_string[i]).\n            result.append(cleaned_string[current_group_start : i + 1])\n            # Update current_group_start to i + 1 to mark the beginning of the next potential group.\n            current_group_start = i + 1\n\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/2", "solution": "def truncate_number(number: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Extracts the decimal part of a positive floating-point number.\n\n    For example:\n    - truncate_number(3.5) returns 0.5\n    - truncate_number(10.99) returns 0.99\n    - truncate_number(5.0) returns 0.0\n\n    Args:\n        number (float): A positive floating-point number.\n\n    Returns:\n        float: The decimal part of the input number.\n    \"\"\"\n    integer_part = int(number)\n    decimal_part = number - integer_part\n    return decimal_part"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/2", "solution": "import math\ndef truncate_number(number: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\" Given a positive floating point number, it can be decomposed into\n    an integer part (largest integer smaller than given number) and decimals\n    (leftover part always smaller than 1).\n\n    Return the decimal part of the number.\n\n    Args:\n        number (float): A positive floating-point number.\n\n    Returns:\n        float: The decimal part of the number.\n\n    Examples:\n    >>> truncate_number(3.5)\n    0.5\n    >>> truncate_number(10.0)\n    0.0\n    >>> truncate_number(0.75)\n    0.75\n    >>> truncate_number(0.0)\n    0.0\n    >>> truncate_number(123.456)\n    0.45600000000000307\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(number, (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'number' must be a float or an integer.\")\n    if number < 0:\n        # As per SRS assumptions, input is a positive float.\n        # However, following the design's example implementation for robustness.\n        raise ValueError(\"Input number must be positive (>= 0).\")\n\n    integer_part = math.floor(number)\n    decimal_part = number - integer_part\n    return decimal_part"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/3", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef below_zero(operations: List[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Detects if at any point the balance of a bank account falls below zero.\n\n    The account starts with a zero balance. Operations are processed sequentially.\n    If the balance falls below zero at any point, the function immediately returns True.\n    Otherwise, if all operations are processed and the balance never falls below zero,\n    the function returns False.\n\n    Args:\n        operations: A list of integer values, where positive values represent deposits\n                    and negative values represent withdrawals.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the balance ever falls below zero, False otherwise.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> below_zero([1, 2, 3])\n        False\n        >>> below_zero([1, 2, -4, 5])\n        True\n        >>> below_zero([-10, 5, 5])\n        True\n        >>> below_zero([10, -5, -3])\n        False\n        >>> below_zero([])\n        False\n    \"\"\"\n    current_balance = 0\n    for op in operations:\n        current_balance += op\n        if current_balance < 0:\n            return True # Balance fell below zero\n    return False # Balance never fell below zero"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/3", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef below_zero(operations: List[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Detects if a bank account balance, starting at zero, ever falls below zero\n    after a sequence of deposit and withdrawal operations.\n\n    The function iterates through the list of operations, updating a running balance.\n    If the balance ever drops below zero at any point, the function immediately\n    returns True. If all operations are processed and the balance never falls\n    below zero, it returns False.\n\n    Args:\n        operations (List[int]): A list of integer operations.\n                                Positive values are deposits, negative are withdrawals.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if the balance falls below zero at any point, False otherwise.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> below_zero([1, 2, 3])\n        False\n        >>> below_zero([1, 2, -4, 5])\n        True\n        >>> below_zero([])\n        False\n        >>> below_zero([-10])\n        True\n        >>> below_zero([5, -3, -3])\n        True\n        >>> below_zero([5, -2, -2, -2])\n        True\n        >>> below_zero([5, -2, -2, 1])\n        False\n    \"\"\"\n    balance = 0\n    for operation in operations:\n        balance += operation\n        if balance < 0:\n            return True\n    return False"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "def mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\" For a given list of input numbers, calculate Mean Absolute Deviation\n    around the mean of this dataset.\n    Mean Absolute Deviation is the average absolute difference between each\n    element and a centerpoint (mean in this case):\n    MAD = average | x - x_mean |\n    >>> mean_absolute_deviation([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0])\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty for mean absolute deviation calculation.\")\n\n    # Calculate the mean of the dataset\n    data_mean = sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\n\n    # Calculate the sum of absolute differences from the mean\n    sum_abs_diff = sum(abs(x - data_mean) for x in numbers)\n\n    # Calculate the mean absolute deviation\n    mad = sum_abs_diff / len(numbers)\n\n    return mad"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/4", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef mean_absolute_deviation(numbers: List[float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\" For a given list of input numbers, calculate Mean Absolute Deviation\n    around the mean of this dataset.\n    Mean Absolute Deviation is the average absolute difference between each\n    element and a centerpoint (mean in this case):\n    MAD = average | x - x_mean |\n    >>> mean_absolute_deviation([1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0])\n    1.0\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input list cannot be empty.\")\n\n    mean = sum(numbers) / len(numbers)\n\n    absolute_differences = [abs(num - mean) for num in numbers]\n\n    mad = sum(absolute_differences) / len(absolute_differences)\n\n    return mad"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef intersperse(numbers: List[int], delimeter: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Inserts a specified delimiter value between every two consecutive elements\n    of an input list of numbers, returning a new list.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers to intersperse.\n        delimeter: An integer value to be inserted between elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of integers with the delimiter inserted.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> intersperse([], 4)\n        []\n        >>> intersperse([1], 4)\n        [1]\n        >>> intersperse([1, 2, 3], 4)\n        [1, 4, 2, 4, 3]\n        >>> intersperse([10, 20], 0)\n        [10, 0, 20]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return []\n\n    result = []\n    for i, num in enumerate(numbers):\n        result.append(num)\n        # Only append the delimiter if it's not the last element\n        if i < len(numbers) - 1:\n            result.append(delimeter)\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/5", "solution": "def intersperse(numbers: list[int], delimeter: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Inserts a delimeter between every two consecutive elements of the input list numbers.\n\n    >>> intersperse([1, 2, 3], 0)\n    [1, 0, 2, 0, 3]\n    >>> intersperse([], 0)\n    []\n    >>> intersperse([1], 0)\n    [1]\n    >>> intersperse([1, 2], -1)\n    [1, -1, 2]\n    >>> intersperse([5, 5, 5], 10)\n    [5, 10, 5, 10, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers or len(numbers) == 1:\n        return list(numbers)  # Return a shallow copy\n\n    result = []\n    for i, num in enumerate(numbers):\n        result.append(num)\n        if i < len(numbers) - 1:\n            result.append(delimeter)\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\" Input to this function is a string represented multiple groups for nested parentheses separated by spaces.\n    For each of the group, output the deepest level of nesting of parentheses.\n    E.g. (()()) has maximum two levels of nesting while ((())) has three.\n\n    >>> parse_nested_parens('(()()) ((())) () ((())()())')\n    [2, 3, 1, 3]\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    groups = paren_string.split(' ')\n\n    for group in groups:\n        current_depth = 0\n        max_depth = 0\n        for char in group:\n            if char == '(':\n                current_depth += 1\n                max_depth = max(max_depth, current_depth)\n            elif char == ')':\n                current_depth -= 1\n        result.append(max_depth)\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/6", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef _calculate_group_depth(group_str: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum nesting depth for a single string of parentheses.\n\n    Args:\n        group_str: A string representing a single group of nested parentheses.\n                   E.g., '(()())' or '((()))'.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the maximum nesting depth for the group.\n        Returns 0 for an empty string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not group_str:\n        return 0\n\n    max_depth = 0\n    current_depth = 0\n    for char in group_str:\n        if char == '(':\n            current_depth += 1\n            max_depth = max(max_depth, current_depth)\n        elif char == ')':\n            current_depth -= 1\n    return max_depth\ndef parse_nested_parens(paren_string: str) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string containing multiple groups of nested parentheses\n    and returns the deepest level of nesting for each group.\n\n    Args:\n        paren_string: A string represented multiple groups for nested parentheses\n                      separated by spaces. E.g., '(()()) ((())) ()'.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of integers, where each integer is the maximum nesting depth\n        of the corresponding group.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> parse_nested_parens('(()()) ((())) () ((())()())')\n        [2, 3, 1, 3]\n    \"\"\"\n    groups = paren_string.split(' ')\n\n    results = []\n    for group_str in groups:\n        results.append(_calculate_group_depth(group_str))\n\n    return results"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/7", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef filter_by_substring(strings: List[str], substring: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Filters an input list of strings, returning only those that contain the given substring.\n    The substring check is case-sensitive.\n\n    Args:\n        strings (List[str]): A list of strings to be filtered.\n        substring (str): The substring to search for within each string.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A new list containing strings from the input list that include the substring.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> filter_by_substring([], 'a')\n        []\n        >>> filter_by_substring(['abc', 'bacd', 'cde', 'array'], 'a')\n        ['abc', 'bacd', 'array']\n        >>> filter_by_substring(['Hello', 'world', 'hEllo'], 'ello')\n        ['Hello']\n    \"\"\"\n    return [s for s in strings if substring in s]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/7", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef filter_by_substring(strings: List[str], substring: str) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Filters a list of strings, returning only those that contain the specified substring.\n    The matching is case-sensitive.\n\n    Args:\n        strings (List[str]): A list of strings to be filtered.\n        substring (str): The substring to search for within each string.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[str]: A new list containing strings from the input that include the substring.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> filter_by_substring([], 'a')\n        []\n        >>> filter_by_substring(['abc', 'bacd', 'cde', 'array'], 'a')\n        ['abc', 'bacd', 'array']\n        >>> filter_by_substring(['Apple', 'apple'], 'A')\n        ['Apple']\n        >>> filter_by_substring(['banana', 'grape'], 'x')\n        []\n    \"\"\"\n    filtered_strings = []\n    for s in strings:\n        if substring in s:\n            filtered_strings.append(s)\n    return filtered_strings"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/8", "solution": "from typing import List, Tuple\ndef sum_product(numbers: List[int]) -> Tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum and product of all integers in a given list.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        A tuple (sum, product) where sum is the total sum of elements\n        and product is the total product of elements.\n        An empty list results in a sum of 0 and a product of 1.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> sum_product([])\n        (0, 1)\n        >>> sum_product([1, 2, 3, 4])\n        (10, 24)\n        >>> sum_product([-1, 0, 1])\n        (0, 0)\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    total_product = 1\n\n    for number in numbers:\n        total_sum += number\n        total_product *= number\n\n    return (total_sum, total_product)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/9", "solution": "from typing import List\ndef rolling_max(numbers: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    From a given list of integers, generate a list of rolling maximum element found until given moment\n    in the sequence.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of integers representing the rolling maximums.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> rolling_max([1, 2, 3, 2, 3, 4, 2])\n        [1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4]\n        >>> rolling_max([])\n        []\n        >>> rolling_max([5])\n        [5]\n        >>> rolling_max([10, 5, 20, 15])\n        [10, 10, 20, 20]\n    \"\"\"\n    result: List[int] = []\n    current_max = float('-inf')  # Initialize to negative infinity to correctly handle all numbers, including negatives\n\n    if not numbers:\n        return result\n\n    for num in numbers:\n        if num > current_max:\n            current_max = num\n        result.append(current_max)\n\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/10", "solution": "def is_palindrome(s: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given string is a palindrome.\n\n    A palindrome is a string that reads the same forwards and backward.\n    The comparison is case-sensitive and includes all characters.\n\n    Args:\n        s: The input string to check.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the string is a palindrome, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    return s == s[::-1]\ndef make_palindrome(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates the shortest palindrome that begins with the supplied string.\n\n    The algorithm finds the longest suffix of the input string that is already\n    a palindrome. It then takes the prefix of the string that comes before this\n    palindromic suffix, reverses it, and appends it to the original string.\n\n    Args:\n        s: The input string from which to construct a palindrome.\n\n    Returns:\n        The shortest palindrome starting with the input string.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not s:\n        return \"\"\n\n    for i in range(len(s)):\n        # Check if the suffix from index i to the end is a palindrome\n        if is_palindrome(s[i:]):\n            # If it is, the prefix to be reversed is the part before this suffix\n            prefix_to_reverse = s[:i]\n            # Append the reversed prefix to the original string to form the palindrome\n            return s + prefix_to_reverse[::-1]\n    \n    # This part should ideally not be reached for a non-empty string,\n    # as at least the last character (a single-character string)\n    # is always a palindrome, ensuring a suffix is found.\n    return s"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/11", "solution": "def string_xor(a: str, b: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Performs a bitwise XOR operation on two binary strings.\n\n    Args:\n        a: The first binary string (consisting of '0's and '1's).\n        b: The second binary string (consisting of '0's and '1's).\n           Assumed to be of the same length as 'a'.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new string representing the result of the bitwise XOR operation.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_list = []\n    # Assuming len(a) == len(b) based on the design document\n    for i in range(len(a)):\n        char_a = a[i]\n        char_b = b[i]\n\n        int_a = int(char_a)\n        int_b = int(char_b)\n\n        xor_result = int_a ^ int_b\n        str_xor_result = str(xor_result)\n        result_list.append(str_xor_result)\n\n    return \"\".join(result_list)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/12", "solution": "from typing import List, Optional\ndef longest(strings: List[str]) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies and returns the longest string from a given list of strings.\n\n    Handles empty input lists by returning None. In case of a tie in length,\n    the string appearing earliest in the input list is returned.\n\n    Args:\n        strings: A list of zero or more strings.\n\n    Returns:\n        The longest string from the list, or the first one encountered in case\n        of a tie. Returns None if the input list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not strings:\n        return None\n\n    longest_so_far = strings[0]\n    max_length = len(longest_so_far)\n\n    for s in strings[1:]:\n        current_length = len(s)\n        if current_length > max_length:\n            max_length = current_length\n            longest_so_far = s\n\n    return longest_so_far"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/12", "solution": "from typing import List, Optional\ndef longest(strings: List[str]) -> Optional[str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies and returns the longest string from a given list of strings.\n    If the list is empty, it returns None.\n    If multiple strings share the maximum length, the first one encountered\n    in the list order is returned.\n\n    Args analogous to:\n        strings: A list of string elements.\n\n    Returns:\n        The longest string from the list, or None if the list is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not strings:\n        return None\n\n    longest_so_far = strings[0]\n    max_length = len(strings[0])\n\n    # Iterate through the strings to find the longest one\n    # The comparison 'len(s) > max_length' ensures that in case of a tie,\n    # the 'longest_so_far' is not updated, preserving the first encountered longest string.\n    for s in strings:\n        if len(s) > max_length:\n            max_length = len(s)\n            longest_so_far = s\n    \n    return longest_so_far"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "def greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two non-negative integers\n    using the Euclidean algorithm.\n\n    Args:\n        a (int): The first non-negative integer.\n        b (int): The second non-negative integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The greatest common divisor of a and b.\n    \"\"\"\n    while b:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    return a"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/13", "solution": "def greatest_common_divisor(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the greatest common divisor (GCD) of two integers using the Euclidean algorithm.\n\n    This function handles both positive and negative integers, as well as zero inputs.\n    The GCD of (a, b) is defined as the largest positive integer that divides both a and b.\n    - If one number is zero and the other is non-zero, the GCD is the absolute value\n      of the non-zero number (e.g., GCD(10, 0) = 10).\n    - If both numbers are zero, the GCD is defined as 0.\n\n    Args:\n        a: The first integer.\n        b: The second integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        The greatest common divisor of a and b.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(3, 5)\n        1\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(25, 15)\n        5\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(10, 0)\n        10\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(0, 10)\n        10\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(0, 0)\n        0\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(-10, 5)\n        5\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(10, -15)\n        5\n        >>> greatest_common_divisor(-10, -15)\n        5\n    \"\"\"\n    # Handle negative inputs by taking absolute values.\n    # The GCD(a, b) is the same as GCD(|a|, |b|).\n    a = abs(a)\n    b = abs(b)\n\n    # Implement the Euclidean algorithm.\n    # The algorithm relies on the property: gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a % b).\n    # The base case is when b becomes 0, then a is the GCD.\n    while b != 0:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    return a"}
