{"task_id": "HumanEval/22", "solution": "from typing import List, Any\ndef filter_integers(values: List[Any]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Filters a list to return a new list containing only integer elements.\n\n    This function iterates through the input list, identifies elements that are\n    instances of the 'int' type (including subclasses like 'bool'), and collects\n    them into a new list. The order of the integers is preserved.\n\n    Args:\n        values (List[Any]): A list containing elements of any Python type.\n\n    Returns:\n        List[int]: A new list containing only the integer values from the input list.\n\n    Examples:\n    >>> filter_integers([1, 'a', 2.5, 3, True, None, {'key': 1}, [4, 5]])\n    [1, 3, True]\n\n    >>> filter_integers([])\n    []\n\n    >>> filter_integers(['hello', 3.14, 'world'])\n    []\n\n    >>> filter_integers([100, -50, 0])\n    [100, -50, 0]\n\n    >>> filter_integers([True, False, 1, 0, 10.0]) # Booleans are subclasses of int\n    [True, False, 1, 0]\n\n    >>> original_list = [1, 'test', 2]\n    >>> result = filter_integers(original_list)\n    >>> result\n    [1, 2]\n    >>> original_list # Ensure original list is not modified\n    [1, 'test', 2]\n    \"\"\"\n    # FUN-001: Accepts a single argument, 'values', which is expected to be a List[Any].\n    # FUN-002: Identifies elements that are instances of the 'int' type (isinstance(item, int)).\n    # FUN-003: Excludes all elements not identified as integers.\n    # FUN-004: Constructs a new list.\n    # FUN-005: Preserves the order of integer elements.\n    # FUN-006: Returns the newly constructed list.\n    # FUN-007: Handles empty input by returning an empty list.\n    # FUN-008: Does not modify the original input list (list comprehension creates a new list).\n    # PERF-001: O(N) time complexity due to single iteration.\n    # PERF-002: O(K) additional space for the result list.\n    return [item for item in values if isinstance(item, int)]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/22", "solution": "from typing import List, Any\ndef filter_integers(values: List[Any]) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Filters a list, returning only integer values.\n\n    Args:\n        values: A list that can contain elements of any type.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list containing only the integer elements from the input list,\n        in their original order.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> filter_integers(['a', 3.14, 5])\n        [5]\n        >>> filter_integers([1, 2, 3, 'abc', {}, []])\n        [1, 2, 3]\n        >>> filter_integers([])\n        []\n        >>> filter_integers([None, 'test', 0.5])\n        []\n    \"\"\"\n    return [item for item in values if isinstance(item, int)]"}
