{"task_id": "HumanEval/31", "solution": "import math\ndef is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns True if a given number is prime, and False otherwise.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The integer number to check for primality.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if n is prime, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # F.2: Numbers less than or equal to 1 are not prime.\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    # F.3: 2 is the only even prime number.\n    if n == 2:\n        return True\n    # F.4: All other even numbers are not prime.\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n\n    # F.5: Check for odd divisors from 3 up to the square root of n.\n    # We only need to check divisors up to sqrt(n) because if n has a divisor\n    # larger than sqrt(n), it must also have a divisor smaller than sqrt(n).\n    # The `+ 1` in `range` makes the upper bound inclusive.\n    limit = int(math.sqrt(n))\n    for d in range(3, limit + 1, 2): # Increment by 2 to check only odd numbers\n        # F.6: If a divisor is found, n is not prime.\n        if n % d == 0:\n            return False\n\n    # F.7: If no divisors are found, n is prime.\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/40", "solution": "def triples_sum_to_zero(l: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if there exist three distinct elements within a list of integers that sum to zero.\n\n    The function first sorts the input list to enable an efficient two-pointer approach.\n    It then iterates through each element, using it as the first of the three numbers.\n    For the remaining two numbers, it uses a two-pointer technique on the rest of the sorted list\n    to find a pair that sums to the negative of the first number.\n\n    Args:\n        l: A Python list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if there are three distinct elements (from different indices) in 'l' that sum to zero,\n        False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(l)\n\n    # F.1 Input Validation: If the list has fewer than 3 elements,\n    # it's impossible to form a triple.\n    if n < 3:\n        return False\n\n    # P.1 Time Complexity: Sort the list. This takes O(N log N) time.\n    # Sorting is crucial for the O(N^2) two-pointer approach.\n    l.sort()\n\n    # Iterate through the list. 'i' represents the index of the first element 'a' in the triple.\n    # We only need to iterate up to n-3 because we need at least two more elements (left, right).\n    for i in range(n - 2):\n        # Optimization: Skip duplicate values for the first element 'a'.\n        # This prevents redundant calculations for identical 'a' values and ensures\n        # that we are effectively considering distinct starting points for the two-pointer search.\n        if i > 0 and l[i] == l[i - 1]:\n            continue\n\n        # F.2 Distinct Element Check: Initialize two pointers for the remaining part of the list.\n        # 'left' pointer starts immediately after 'i'.\n        # 'right' pointer starts at the end of the list.\n        left = i + 1\n        right = n - 1\n\n        # The two-pointer loop continues as long as 'left' is less than 'right',\n        # ensuring that 'left' and 'right' point to distinct elements and are\n        # also distinct from 'i'.\n        while left < right:\n            # F.3 Summation Logic: Calculate the sum of the three distinct elements.\n            current_sum = l[i] + l[left] + l[right]\n\n            # F.4 Zero Sum Detection:\n            if current_sum == 0:\n                # F.5 Return True: If the sum is zero, a valid triple has been found.\n                # The elements l[i], l[left], l[right] are guaranteed to be from\n                # distinct indices (i < left < right).\n                return True\n            # If the current sum is less than zero, it means we need a larger sum.\n            # Move the 'left' pointer to the right to pick a larger second element.\n            elif current_sum < 0:\n                left += 1\n            # If the current sum is greater than zero, it means we need a smaller sum.\n            # Move the 'right' pointer to the left to pick a smaller third element.\n            else:  # current_sum > 0\n                right -= 1\n\n            # Note: For this problem, which asks only for the *existence* of such a triple\n            # and returns immediately upon finding one, explicit duplicate skipping\n            # for `l[left]` and `l[right]` *after* adjusting pointers is not strictly\n            # necessary. The `l[i]` duplicate skip is sufficient for performance\n            # and the `i < left < right` condition ensures distinct indices.\n\n    # F.6 Return False: If the loops complete without finding any triple that sums to zero.\n    return False"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/41", "solution": "def car_race_collision(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of collisions between two sets of n cars.\n\n    Imagine a road that's a perfectly straight infinitely long line.\n    n cars are driving left to right; simultaneously, a different set of n cars\n    are driving right to left. The two sets of cars start out being very far from\n    each other. All cars move in the same speed. Two cars are said to collide\n    when a car that's moving left to right hits a car that's moving right to left.\n    However, the cars are infinitely sturdy and strong; as a result, they continue moving\n    in their trajectory as if they did not collide.\n\n    Args:\n        n: The number of cars moving left-to-right and\n           the number of cars moving right-to-left.\n           If n is negative, it is treated as 0 cars, resulting in 0 collisions.\n\n    Returns:\n        The total number of collisions.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        return 0\n    return n * n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/41", "solution": "def car_race_collision(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of collision events between two sets of n cars\n    moving in opposite directions on an infinitely long line.\n\n    All cars move at the same speed and pass through each other as if\n    no collision occurred.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The number of cars in each of the two opposing sets.\n                 Must be a non-negative integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The total number of collision events (n * n).\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is a negative integer or not an integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    # Each car from one set will collide with every car from the other set.\n    # Number of cars in set 1 = n\n    # Number of cars in set 2 = n\n    # Total collisions = n * n\n    return n * n"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/42", "solution": "from typing import List, Union\ndef incr_list(numbers: List[Union[int, float]]) -> List[Union[int, float]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Increments each numeric element in a list by 1 and returns a new list.\n\n    The original input list remains unmodified.\n\n    Args:\n        numbers: A list of numbers (integers or floats).\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list with each element incremented by 1.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If the input is not a list or if elements within the list are\n                   not numeric (int or float).\n                   (Implicitly raised by Python's type system during operations)\n    \n    Example:\n        >>> incr_list([1, 2, 3])\n        [2, 3, 4]\n        >>> incr_list([0.5, 1.5, 2.0])\n        [1.5, 2.5, 3.0]\n        >>> original = [10, 20]\n        >>> new_list = incr_list(original)\n        >>> new_list\n        [11, 21]\n        >>> original # Original list is unchanged\n        [10, 20]\n    \"\"\"\n    # Using a list comprehension for a concise and efficient implementation\n    # This creates a new list without modifying the original.\n    return [num + 1 for num in numbers]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/42", "solution": "def incr_list(l: list) -> list:\n    \"\"\"\n    Returns a new list with elements from the input list incremented by 1.\n\n    The original list remains unchanged.\n\n    Args:\n        l (list): A list of numeric elements (integers or floats).\n\n    Returns:\n        list: A new list with each element incremented by 1.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If any element in the input list is not numeric.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> incr_list([1, 2, 3])\n        [2, 3, 4]\n        >>> incr_list([])\n        []\n        >>> incr_list([1.5, 2.0])\n        [2.5, 3.0]\n    \"\"\"\n    # REQ-001: Accept a list `l`\n    # REQ-008: Error Handling (Non-numeric) - check all elements first\n    for item in l:\n        if not isinstance(item, (int, float)):\n            raise TypeError(\"All elements in the list must be numeric (int or float).\")\n\n    # REQ-002: Element Iteration\n    # REQ-003: Element Increment\n    # REQ-004: New List Return\n    # REQ-005: Immutability of Input (list comprehension creates a new list)\n    # REQ-006: Empty List Handling (list comprehension on empty list returns empty list)\n    # REQ-007: Numeric Type Handling (int and float addition works as expected)\n    # REQ-009: Time Complexity (O(n) - two passes, both O(n))\\n    # REQ-010: Consistent Output\n    # REQ-013: Readability\n    # REQ-014: Docstrings and Type Hints\n    return [item + 1 for item in l]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/43", "solution": "class ZeroSumFinderService:\n    def pairs_sum_to_zero(self, numbers: list[int]) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if there are two distinct elements in the input list that sum to zero.\n\n        Args:\n            numbers: A list of integers.\n\n        Returns:\n            True if such a pair exists, False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        # FR2: Handle Empty/Single Element List\n        if len(numbers) < 2:\n            return False\n\n        # FR3, FR4, FR5, and 3.3.1 Performance/Space: Use a hash set for O(N) average time complexity.\n        seen_numbers = set()\n\n        for number in numbers:\n            complement = -number\n            # FR4: Zero Sum Detection - check if the complement has been seen\n            if complement in seen_numbers:\n                return True\n            # Add the current number to the set for future checks\n            seen_numbers.add(number)\n\n        # FR5: No Zero Sum Pair - if loop completes without finding a pair\n        return False"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/43", "solution": "def pairs_sum_to_zero(l):\n    \"\"\"\n    pairs_sum_to_zero takes a list of integers as an input.\n    it returns True if there are two distinct elements in the list that\n    sum to zero, and False otherwise.\n\n    The implementation uses a set to store numbers encountered so far,\n    achieving an average time complexity of O(N) and space complexity of O(N),\n    where N is the length of the input list.\n\n    Args:\n        l (list[int]): A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if two distinct elements sum to zero, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(l) < 2:\n        return False\n\n    seen_numbers = set()\n    for num in l:\n        complement = -num\n        if complement in seen_numbers:\n            return True\n        seen_numbers.add(num)\n    return False"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/44", "solution": "def change_base(x: int, base: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a non-negative integer from base-10 to a string representation\n    in a specified target base.\n\n    This function is constrained to handle target bases less than 10 (i.e., 2 <= base < 10).\n\n    Args:\n        x (int): The non-negative integer number in base 10 to be converted.\n        base (int): The target base for the conversion, an integer such that\n                    2 <= base < 10.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: A string representing the number x in the specified base.\n\n    Example:\n        >>> change_base(8, 3)\n        '22'\n        >>> change_base(10, 2)\n        '1010'\n        >>> change_base(0, 7)\n        '0'\n        >>> change_base(15, 8)\n        '17'\n    \"\"\"\n    if x == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n\n    # Use a list to store digits and append them. This is generally more efficient\n    # than repeated string concatenation. The digits will be collected in reverse order.\n    result_digits = []\n\n    while x > 0:\n        remainder = x % base\n        result_digits.append(str(remainder))\n        x = x // base\n\n    # The collected digits are in reverse order (least significant first),\n    # so we reverse the list to get the correct order (most significant first).\n    result_digits.reverse()\n\n    # Join the list of digit strings into a single result string.\n    return \"\".join(result_digits)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/44", "solution": "def change_base(x: int, base: int) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a non-negative integer x from base 10 to the specified base.\n\n    Args:\n        x (int): The non-negative integer to convert (base 10).\n        base (int): The target base (an integer, 2 <= base <= 9).\n\n    Returns:\n        str: The string representation of x in the specified base.\n    \"\"\"\n    if x == 0:\n        return \"0\"\n\n    # The requirements state that 'base' will always be within the valid range (2-9),\n    # so explicit validation (e.g., raising ValueError) is not strictly required by FR-3\n    # which says \"The system shall accept base as an integer where 2 <= base <= 9\",\n    # implying valid input.\n    # However, if one were to add robustness beyond the strict interpretation of \"accept\",\n    # it might look like:\n    # if not (2 <= base <= 9):\n    #     raise ValueError(\"Target base must be an integer between 2 and 9, inclusive.\")\n    # if x < 0:\n    #     raise ValueError(\"Input number x must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    result = []\n    current_x = x\n\n    while current_x > 0:\n        remainder = current_x % base\n        result.append(str(remainder))\n        current_x //= base\n\n    # The digits are collected in reverse order, so we need to reverse the list\n    # and join them to form the final string.\n    return \"\".join(result[::-1])"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/45", "solution": "def triangle_area(a, h):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given length of a side and height return area for a triangle.\n\n    Parameters:\n    a (float or int): The base length of the triangle.\n    h (float or int): The height of the triangle.\n\n    Returns:\n    float: The calculated area of the triangle.\n\n    >>> triangle_area(5, 3)\n    7.5\n    >>> triangle_area(10.0, 4)\n    20.0\n    >>> triangle_area(7, 2.5)\n    8.75\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(a, (int, float)) or not isinstance(h, (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError(\"Both 'a' and 'h' must be numerical values.\")\n    \n    # The core calculation\n    area = 0.5 * a * h\n    return float(area)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/45", "solution": "from typing import Union\ndef triangle_area(a: Union[int, float], h: Union[int, float]) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the area of a triangle given its base and height.\n\n    Args:\n        a: The length of the base of the triangle.\n        h: The height of the triangle.\n\n    Returns:\n        The calculated area of the triangle as a float.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If inputs 'a' or 'h' are not numeric (int or float).\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> triangle_area(5, 3)\n        7.5\n        >>> triangle_area(10, 4)\n        20.0\n        >>> triangle_area(2.5, 4.0)\n        5.0\n        >>> triangle_area(0, 5)\n        0.0\n        >>> triangle_area(5, 0)\n        0.0\n        >>> triangle_area(-5, 3) # Mathematically calculated, but geometrically invalid input\n        -7.5\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(a, (int, float)) or not isinstance(h, (int, float)):\n        raise TypeError(\"Inputs 'a' and 'h' must be numeric.\")\n    \n    if a < 0 or h < 0:\n        # As per SRS 2.5 (\"Assumptions and Dependencies\"), the function performs\n        # the arithmetic operation regardless of negative values, but the\n        # geometric interpretation is lost. No specific error is raised here\n        # for negative values, as the calculation itself remains valid.\n        pass \n        \n    return 0.5 * a * h"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/46", "solution": "def fib4(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Efficiently computes the n-th element of the Fib4 sequence without using recursion.\n\n    The sequence is defined as:\n    fib4(0) -> 0\n    fib4(1) -> 0\n    fib4(2) -> 2\n    fib4(3) -> 0\n    fib4(n) -> fib4(n-1) + fib4(n-2) + fib4(n-3) + fib4(n-4) for n >= 4.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The index of the desired Fib4 number (must be non-negative).\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The n-th Fib4 number.\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If n is not an integer.\n        ValueError: If n is a negative integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return 0\n    if n == 2:\n        return 2\n    if n == 3:\n        return 0\n\n    # Initialize a list to store computed values (dynamic programming - tabulation)\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n\n    # Set base cases\n    dp[0] = 0\n    dp[1] = 0\n    dp[2] = 2\n    dp[3] = 0\n\n    # Compute subsequent values iteratively\n    for i in range(4, n + 1):\n        dp[i] = dp[i-1] + dp[i-2] + dp[i-3] + dp[i-4]\n\n    return dp[n]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/46", "solution": "def fib4(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the n-th element of the Fib4 sequence iteratively.\n\n    The sequence is defined as:\n    fib4(0) = 0\n    fib4(1) = 0\n    fib4(2) = 2\n    fib4(3) = 0\n    fib4(n) = fib4(n-1) + fib4(n-2) + fib4(n-3) + fib4(n-4) for n > 3.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The index of the Fib4 sequence element to compute.\n                 Must be a non-negative integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The n-th element of the Fib4 sequence.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is a negative integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return 0\n    if n == 2:\n        return 2\n    if n == 3:\n        return 0\n\n    # For n > 3, use an iterative approach\n    # We need to store the last four values to compute the next one\n    # Let a, b, c, d be fib4(i-4), fib4(i-3), fib4(i-2), fib4(i-1) respectively\n    # Initialize with the base cases for n=0, 1, 2, 3\n    # fib4(0) corresponds to 'a'\n    # fib4(1) corresponds to 'b'\n    # fib4(2) corresponds to 'c'\n    # fib4(3) corresponds to 'd'\n\n    a, b, c, d = 0, 0, 2, 0\n\n    # Iterate from 4 up to n\n    for i in range(4, n + 1):\n        next_fib4 = a + b + c + d\n        # Shift values: (a, b, c, d) becomes (b, c, d, next_fib4)\n        a = b\n        b = c\n        c = d\n        d = next_fib4\n\n    return d"}
