{"task_id": "HumanEval/49", "solution": "def modp(n: int, p: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates (2^n) % p efficiently using modular exponentiation.\n\n    This function computes the remainder of 2 raised to the power of n when\n    divided by p. It uses the \"exponentiation by squaring\" algorithm to ensure\n    logarithmic time complexity (O(log n)) and constant auxiliary space (O(1)),\n    making it suitable for very large exponents n.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The non-negative integer exponent.\n                 Must be n >= 0.\n        p (int): The positive integer modulus.\n                 Must be p > 0.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The result of (2^n) % p, an integer in the range [0, p-1].\n\n    Raises:\n        TypeError: If n or p are not integers.\n        ValueError: If n is negative or p is not positive.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> modp(3, 5)\n        3\n        >>> modp(1101, 101)\n        2\n        >>> modp(0, 101)\n        1\n        >>> modp(3, 11)\n        8\n        >>> modp(100, 101)\n        1\n        >>> modp(5, 1)\n        0\n        >>> modp(10**18, 7) # Example with a very large exponent\n        1\n    \"\"\"\n    # Type validation\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or not isinstance(p, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Both n and p must be integers.\")\n\n    # Input validation as per product constraints\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Exponent n must be non-negative.\")\n    if p <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Modulus p must be positive.\")\n\n    # REQ-FUNC-003: Handle p=1. Any number modulo 1 is 0.\n    if p == 1:\n        return 0\n\n    # REQ-FUNC-002: Handle n=0. 2^0 = 1. (This case is for p > 1, as p=1 is handled above).\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # REQ-FUNC-001 & QoS-PERF-001: Implement modular exponentiation by squaring.\n    # Initialize result and base.\n    res = 1\n    # Initialize base as 2, and immediately take modulo p to keep numbers small.\n    # This also correctly handles cases where 2 is a multiple of p (e.g., p=2).\n    base = 2 % p\n\n    # Iterate while the exponent n is greater than 0\n    while n > 0:\n        # If n is odd, multiply the current result by the base and take modulo p\n        if n % 2 == 1:\n            res = (res * base) % p\n        \n        # Square the base and take modulo p\n        base = (base * base) % p\n        \n        # Halve the exponent n\n        n //= 2\n    \n    return res"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/50", "solution": "def decode_shift(s: str):\n    \"\"\"\n    Decodes a string that has been encoded using a +5 shift cipher.\n    Each lowercase character is shifted back by 5 positions, wrapping around\n    from 'a' to 'z'. Non-lowercase characters remain unchanged.\n\n    Args:\n        s (str): The string to be decoded.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: The original, decoded string.\n    \"\"\"\n    decoded_chars = []\n    for char in s:\n        if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n            # Calculate the 0-indexed position of the character ('a' is 0, 'b' is 1, ..., 'z' is 25)\n            char_code = ord(char) - ord('a')\n            \n            # Shift back by 5 positions. Use modulo 26 to handle wrap-around from 'a' to 'z'.\n            # Adding 26 before subtracting ensures that the result of (char_code - 5) is positive\n            # before applying modulo, which is important for Python's % operator with negative numbers.\n            # Example: 'e' (4) - 5 = -1. (-1 + 26) % 26 = 25 ('z')\n            # Example: 'f' (5) - 5 = 0. (0 + 26) % 26 = 0 ('a')\n            decoded_code = (char_code - 5 + 26) % 26\n            \n            # Convert the new 0-indexed position back to a character\n            decoded_char = chr(ord('a') + decoded_code)\n            decoded_chars.append(decoded_char)\n        else:\n            # Non-lowercase characters remain unchanged\n            decoded_chars.append(char)\n            \n    return \"\".join(decoded_chars)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/50", "solution": "def decode_shift(s: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Decodes a string that was previously encoded by shifting each alphabetic\n    character 5 positions forward in the alphabet (wrapping 'z' to 'a').\n    This function reverses the operation by shifting each character 5 positions\n    backward, wrapping 'a' to 'z'. Non-alphabetic characters are preserved.\n\n    Args:\n        s: The string to be decoded.\n\n    Returns:\n        The decoded string.\n    \"\"\"\n    decoded_chars = []\n    for char in s:\n        if 'a' <= char <= 'z':  # Lowercase letters\n            # Calculate position relative to 'a' (0-25)\n            char_offset = ord(char) - ord('a')\n            # Shift backward by 5, handle wrapping with modulo 26\n            shifted_offset = (char_offset - 5) % 26\n            # Convert back to character\n            decoded_char = chr(ord('a') + shifted_offset)\n            decoded_chars.append(decoded_char)\n        elif 'A' <= char <= 'Z':  # Uppercase letters\n            # Calculate position relative to 'A' (0-25)\n            char_offset = ord(char) - ord('A')\n            # Shift backward by 5, handle wrapping with modulo 26\n            shifted_offset = (char_offset - 5) % 26\n            # Convert back to character\n            decoded_char = chr(ord('A') + shifted_offset)\n            decoded_chars.append(decoded_char)\n        else:\n            # Non-alphabetic characters remain unchanged\n            decoded_chars.append(char)\n    return \"\".join(decoded_chars)"}
