{"task_id": "HumanEval/121", "solution": "def solution(lst: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a non-empty list of integers, return the sum of all of the odd elements that are in even positions.\n\n    Examples:\n    solution([5, 8, 7, 1]) == 12\n    solution([3, 3, 3, 3, 3]) == 9\n    solution([30, 13, 24, 321]) == 0\n\n    Args:\n        lst (list): A non-empty list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The sum of odd elements found at even positions.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    # Iterate through the list with both index and value\n    for i, element in enumerate(lst):\n        # Check if the current position (index) is even\n        if i % 2 == 0:\n            # Check if the element at this even position is odd\n            if element % 2 != 0:\n                # If both conditions are met, add the element to the total sum\n                total_sum += element\n    return total_sum"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/121", "solution": "def solution(lst: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of all odd elements located at even 0-based positions\n    within a given non-empty list of integers.\n\n    Args:\n        lst: A non-empty list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the sum of all odd elements found at even\n        0-based positions in the input list.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    for index, element in enumerate(lst):\n        # Check if the index is even (0-based)\n        if index % 2 == 0:\n            # Check if the element at this even index is odd\n            if element % 2 != 0:\n                total_sum += element\n    return total_sum"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/122", "solution": "def add_elements(arr: list[int], k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a non-empty array of integers arr and an integer k, return\n    the sum of the elements with at most two digits from the first k elements of arr.\n\n    An element is considered to have 'at most two digits' if its absolute value\n    is less than 100 (i.e., -99 <= element <= 99).\n\n    Args:\n        arr (list[int]): A non-empty list of integers.\n                         Constraint: 1 <= len(arr) <= 100\n        k (int): An integer representing the number of initial elements to consider.\n                 Constraint: 1 <= k <= len(arr)\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The sum of elements with at most two digits from the first k elements of arr.\n\n    Example:\n        Input: arr = [111, 21, 3, 4000, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9], k = 4\n        Output: 24  # sum of 21 + 3\n    \"\"\"\n    current_sum = 0\n    # Iterate only up to the first k elements\n    for i in range(k):\n        element = arr[i]\n        # Check if the element has at most two digits\n        # This means its absolute value must be less than 100\n        if abs(element) < 100:\n            current_sum += element\n            \n    return current_sum"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/122", "solution": "def add_elements(arr, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a non-empty array of integers arr and an integer k, return\n    the sum of the elements with at most two digits from the first k elements of arr.\n\n    Example:\n\n        Input: arr = [111,21,3,4000,5,6,7,8,9], k = 4\n        Output: 24 # sum of 21 + 3\n\n    Constraints:\n        1. 1 <= len(arr) <= 100\n        2. 1 <= k <= len(arr)\n    \"\"\"\n    current_sum = 0\n    # Iterate through the first k elements\n    for i in range(k):\n        element = arr[i]\n        # Check if the element has at most two digits (i.e., its absolute value is less than 100)\n        if abs(element) < 100:\n            current_sum += element\n    return current_sum"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/123", "solution": "def get_odd_collatz(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Collatz sequence for a given positive integer n,\n    extracts all odd numbers encountered, and returns them as a sorted list.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): A positive integer to start the Collatz sequence.\n\n    Returns:\n        list[int]: A sorted list of unique odd numbers from the Collatz sequence.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is not a positive integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR-1: The system shall accept a single positive integer `n` as input.\n    # Input validation for n (Design Step 1)\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    # Design Step 2: Initialization\n    odd_numbers = set()  # Using a set to ensure unique odd numbers (FR-4)\n    current_num = n\n\n    # FR-2: Generate the Collatz sequence\n    # Design Step 3: Collatz Sequence Generation Loop\n    while current_num != 1:  # FR-2.3: Sequence generation terminates when current number reaches 1\n        # FR-3: Identify all odd numbers encountered\n        # Add current_num if it's odd, before applying the rule\n        if current_num % 2 != 0:\n            odd_numbers.add(current_num)\n\n        # Apply Collatz rule (FR-2.1, FR-2.2)\n        if current_num % 2 == 0:  # If even\n            current_num //= 2  # FR-2.1: current_number / 2\n        else:  # If odd\n            current_num = (3 * current_num) + 1  # FR-2.2: 3 * current_number + 1\n\n    # Design Step 4: Final Odd Number (1)\n    # The loop terminates when current_num *becomes* 1, so 1 itself must be added.\n    odd_numbers.add(1)\n\n    # Design Step 5: Result Preparation\n    # Convert the set to a list and sort it (FR-5)\n    result_list = sorted(list(odd_numbers))\n\n    # FR-6: Return the sorted list of unique odd numbers.\n    # Design Step 6: Return Value\n    return result_list"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/123", "solution": "def get_odd_collatz(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Collatz sequence for a given positive integer `n`,\n    identifies all unique odd numbers within that sequence, and returns\n    them as a sorted list.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): A positive integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        list[int]: A sorted list of unique odd integers found in the\n                   Collatz sequence generated from `n`. The list will\n                   be sorted in increasing order.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    current_num = n\n    odd_numbers_set = set()\n\n    while current_num != 1:\n        if current_num % 2 != 0:  # current_num is odd\n            odd_numbers_set.add(current_num)\n            current_num = 3 * current_num + 1\n        else:  # current_num is even\n            current_num = current_num // 2\n    \n    # Add 1 to the set, as it's always part of the sequence and is odd.\n    # This also handles the base case n=1 correctly.\n    odd_numbers_set.add(1)\n\n    result_list = list(odd_numbers_set)\n    result_list.sort()\n\n    return result_list"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/124", "solution": "def valid_date(date_string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates a date string according to the \"mm-dd-yyyy\" format and specific rules.\n\n    Args:\n        date_string: The date to be validated, expected as a string.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the date_string is a valid date according to all specified rules,\n        False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # REQ-FUNC-3: Return False if date_string is empty.\n    if not date_string:\n        return False\n\n    # REQ-FUNC-4: Check format \"mm-dd-yyyy\"\n    # Incorrect delimiters or too many/few parts\n    parts = date_string.split('-')\n    if len(parts) != 3:\n        return False\n\n    mm_str, dd_str, yyyy_str = parts\n\n    # Check length and if parts are digits\n    # Non-numeric parts where numbers are expected, or incorrect lengths\n    if not (len(mm_str) == 2 and mm_str.isdigit() and\n            len(dd_str) == 2 and dd_str.isdigit() and\n            len(yyyy_str) == 4 and yyyy_str.isdigit()):\n        return False\n\n    try:\n        # REQ-FUNC-5: Parse mm, dd, yyyy components from date_string\n        month = int(mm_str)\n        day = int(dd_str)\n        year = int(yyyy_str) # Year is parsed but not used for validation, as per constraints.\n    except ValueError:\n        # This case should largely be covered by isdigit() checks,\n        # but serves as a safeguard against potential edge cases like '-01-01'\n        return False\n\n    # REQ-FUNC-6: Validate month component (1-12)\n    if not (1 <= month <= 12):\n        return False\n\n    # REQ-FUNC-7: Validate day component (>= 1)\n    if day < 1:\n        return False\n\n    # Define days in each month. February is 29 days as per constraints (no leap year logic).\n    days_in_month = {\n        1: 31, 3: 31, 5: 31, 7: 31, 8: 31, 10: 31, 12: 31, # REQ-FUNC-8\n        4: 30, 6: 30, 9: 30, 11: 30,                         # REQ-FUNC-9\n        2: 29                                              # REQ-FUNC-10 (Feb is max 29 days)\n    }\n\n    # REQ-FUNC-8, REQ-FUNC-9, REQ-FUNC-10: Validate day component based on the month\n    # Using .get(month, 0) handles cases where 'month' might somehow be invalid (though\n    # the 1 <= month <= 12 check should prevent this). If month is invalid, it defaults\n    # to 0, making 'day > 0' always true and thus returning False.\n    if day > days_in_month.get(month, 0):\n        return False\n\n    # If all checks pass\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/124", "solution": "class DateValidator:\n    \"\"\"\n    A utility class for validating date strings.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Mapping of month numbers to their maximum allowed days.\n    # February is fixed at 29 days as per requirements, without complex leap year logic.\n    MONTH_DAYS_MAP = {\n        1: 31, 3: 31, 5: 31, 7: 31, 8: 31, 10: 31, 12: 31,  # 31-day months\n        4: 30, 6: 30, 9: 30, 11: 30,                        # 30-day months\n        2: 29                                              # February\n    }\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def valid_date(date_string: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates a date string against the 'mm-dd-yyyy' format and calendar rules.\n\n        Rules:\n        1. Date string must not be empty.\n        2. Date string must strictly follow the 'mm-dd-yyyy' format.\n        3. Month component (mm) must be an integer between 1 and 12, inclusive.\n        4. Day component (dd) must be an integer within the correct range for the given month:\n           - 1-31 for months 1, 3, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12.\n           - 1-30 for months 4, 6, 9, 11.\n           - 1-29 for month 2 (February). No leap year calculation required.\n\n        Args:\n            date_string (str): The date string to validate (e.g., '03-11-2000').\n\n        Returns:\n            bool: True if the date string is valid, False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        # FR-1: Check for empty date string.\n        if not date_string:\n            return False\n\n        # FR-2: Basic format check (length and hyphen positions).\n        # Expected length for 'mm-dd-yyyy' is 10 characters.\n        if len(date_string) != 10 or date_string[2] != '-' or date_string[5] != '-':\n            return False\n\n        # Extract month, day, and year substrings.\n        mm_str = date_string[0:2]\n        dd_str = date_string[3:5]\n        yyyy_str = date_string[6:10]\n\n        # FR-3: Attempt to convert components to integers.\n        # This also implicitly checks if the segments are purely numeric.\n        try:\n            month = int(mm_str)\n            day = int(dd_str)\n            year = int(yyyy_str) # Year is parsed but no specific range validation is required.\n        except ValueError:\n            # If any segment cannot be converted to an integer, it's invalid.\n            return False\n\n        # FR-4: Validate month range (1-12).\n        if not (1 <= month <= 12):\n            return False\n\n        # FR-5, FR-6, FR-7: Validate day range based on the month.\n        # Retrieve the maximum days for the current month from the predefined map.\n        max_days = DateValidator.MONTH_DAYS_MAP.get(month)\n\n        # If month is valid (1-12), it must exist in MONTH_DAYS_MAP.\n        # This check acts as a safeguard, though unlikely to be triggered given previous month validation.\n        if max_days is None:\n            return False\n\n        # Check if the day is within the valid range for the determined month.\n        if not (1 <= day <= max_days):\n            return False\n\n        # FR-8: All checks passed, the date string is valid.\n        return True\n\n# If you need to expose the function directly (e.g., for backwards compatibility or a specific API),\n# you can alias it, though the design specified a class method.\n# For example:\n# valid_date = DateValidator.valid_date"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/125", "solution": "from typing import Union\ndef split_words(txt: str) -> Union[list[str], int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a string of words, return a list of words split on whitespace, if no whitespaces exists in the text you\n    should split on commas ',', if no commas exists you should return the number of lower-case letters with odd order in the\n    alphabet, ord('a') = 0, ord('b') = 1, ... ord('z') = 25\n\n    Args:\n        txt (str): The input string to process.\n\n    Returns:\n        Union[list[str], int]: A list of words if split, or an integer count.\n\n    Examples:\n        >>> split_words(\"Hello world!\")\n        ['Hello', 'world!']\n        >>> split_words(\"Hello,world!\")\n        ['Hello', 'world!']\n        >>> split_words(\"abcdef\")\n        3\n        >>> split_words(\"python\") # 'p'(15)-odd, 'y'(24)-even, 't'(19)-odd, 'h'(7)-odd, 'o'(14)-even, 'n'(13)-odd. Count = 4\n        4\n        >>> split_words(\"no_split_or_comma\")\n        9\n        >>> split_words(\"no-split-or-comma_\") # '-' and '_' are ignored\n        9\n        >>> split_words(\"\")\n        0\n        >>> split_words(\"ABC\")\n        0\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR2: Whitespace-based Splitting - check if any character in the string is whitespace\n    if any(char.isspace() for char in txt):\n        # Using str.split() without arguments handles multiple spaces and leading/trailing spaces\n        return txt.split()\n\n    # FR3: Comma-based Splitting - check if a comma exists in the string\n    elif ',' in txt:\n        return txt.split(',')\n\n    # FR4: Character Counting Fallback - if no whitespace or comma, count specific lowercase letters\n    else:\n        odd_order_count = 0\n        # Iterate through each character in the input string\n        for char in txt:\n            # Check if the character is a lowercase English letter (a-z)\n            if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n                # Calculate the 0-indexed ordinal value ('a' is 0, 'b' is 1, etc.)\n                ordinal_value = ord(char) - ord('a')\n                # Check if the ordinal value is odd\n                if ordinal_value % 2 != 0:\n                    odd_order_count += 1\n        return odd_order_count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/125", "solution": "import re\ndef split_words(txt: str) -> list[str] | int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a string of words, return a list of words split on whitespace, if no whitespaces exists in the text you\n    should split on commas ',', if no commas exists you should return the number of lower-case letters with odd order in the\n    alphabet, ord('a') = 0, ord('b') = 1, ... ord('z') = 25\n\n    Examples\n    split_words(\"Hello world!\") \u279e [\"Hello\", \"world!\"]\n    split_words(\"Hello,world!\") \u279e [\"Hello\", \"world!\"]\n    split_words(\"abcdef\") == 3\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR2: Whitespace Splitting\n    # Check if txt contains one or more whitespace characters\n    if re.search(r'\\s', txt):\n        # Split txt by any sequence of whitespace characters and return a list of non-empty substrings.\n        return txt.split()\n\n    # FR3: Comma Splitting (Fallback)\n    # If txt does not contain any whitespace characters, but contains one or more comma (',') characters\n    if ',' in txt:\n        # Split txt by commas and return a list of the resulting substrings.\n        return txt.split(',')\n\n    # FR4: Character Counting (Fallback)\n    # If txt contains neither whitespace nor comma characters\n    odd_order_count = 0\n    for char in txt:\n        # Check if the character is a lowercase English letter ('a' through 'z')\n        if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n            # Calculate the 0-indexed alphabetical order\n            # ord('a') corresponds to 0, ord('b') to 1, ..., ord('z') to 25\n            ordinal_value = ord(char) - ord('a')\n            # Check if the alphabetical order is an odd number\n            if ordinal_value % 2 != 0:\n                odd_order_count += 1\n    # FR5: Return Value (integer count)\n    return odd_order_count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/126", "solution": "import collections\nfrom typing import List\ndef is_sorted(lst: List[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a list of non-negative integers, determines whether or not it is sorted\n    in ascending order and if no number appears more than twice.\n\n    Args:\n        lst (List[int]): The input list of non-negative integers to be validated.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if the list is sorted in ascending order and no number appears\n              more than twice; False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR-2: Return True if lst is empty or contains a single element.\n    if not lst or len(lst) == 1:\n        return True\n\n    # FR-4: The function shall count the occurrences of each unique number in lst.\n    counts = collections.Counter()\n\n    # Iterate through the list to check both sorting and duplicate constraints\n    # FR-3: Check for ascending order\n    # FR-5: Check for duplicate constraint (no number appears more than twice)\n    for i in range(len(lst)):\n        current_num = lst[i]\n\n        # Check sorting order (FR-3)\n        # This check is only relevant for elements beyond the first one\n        if i > 0:\n            prev_num = lst[i-1]\n            if current_num < prev_num:\n                return False # List is not sorted in ascending order\n\n        # Update and check duplicate constraint (FR-4, FR-5)\n        counts[current_num] += 1\n        if counts[current_num] > 2:\n            return False # Number appears more than twice\n\n    # FR-6: Return True only if both conditions (ascending order and duplicate constraint) are met.\n    # If the loop completes, it means no violations were found.\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/126", "solution": "import collections\ndef is_sorted(lst: list[int]) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a list of non-negative integers is sorted in ascending order\n    and contains at most one duplicate of any number.\n\n    Args:\n        lst: A list of non-negative integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the list meets both criteria, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(lst)\n\n    # FR2, FR3: Handle empty or single-element lists\n    if n <= 1:\n        return True\n\n    # Use collections.Counter for efficient frequency counting\n    # This allows for checking counts in O(1) average time.\n    # PER2: Space complexity is O(U) where U is the number of unique elements.\n    counts = collections.Counter()\n\n    # PER1: Iterate through the list once to check both sorting and duplicate count.\n    # Time complexity is O(N) where N is the length of the list.\n    for i in range(n):\n        current_element = lst[i]\n\n        # Update count for the current element\n        counts[current_element] += 1\n\n        # FR7: Check duplicate count rule (more than one duplicate means count >= 3)\n        if counts[current_element] > 2:\n            return False\n\n        # FR5: Check ascending order for non-last element (lst[i] <= lst[i+1])\n        if i < n - 1:\n            next_element = lst[i+1]\n            if current_element > next_element:\n                return False\n\n    # FR4, FR6, FR8: If the loop completes without returning False,\n    # the list is sorted in ascending order and adheres to the duplicate rule.\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/127", "solution": "import math\nclass IntervalAnalyzer:\n    \"\"\"\n    A utility class to analyze intervals, calculate their intersection,\n    and determine properties of the intersection length.\n    Encapsulates helper logic for primality testing and interval intersection.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        F.4: Determines if a given integer is a prime number.\n        A prime number is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself.\n        \"\"\"\n        if n <= 1:  # F.4 Process 1: If length <= 1, it is not prime.\n            return False\n        if n == 2:  # F.4 Process 2: If length == 2, it is prime.\n            return True\n        if n % 2 == 0: # F.4 Process 3: If length > 2 and length % 2 == 0, it is not prime.\n            return False\n        \n        # F.4 Process 4: For length > 2 and odd, check divisibility by odd numbers from 3 up to the square root of length.\n        # If any divisor is found, it is not prime.\n        # F.4 Process 5: Otherwise, it is prime.\n        for i in range(3, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1, 2):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                return False\n        return True\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def _calculate_intersection_length(interval1: tuple[int, int], interval2: tuple[int, int]) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        F.1: Calculates the intersection of two closed integer intervals.\n        F.2: Determines if a valid intersection exists.\n        F.3: Calculates the length of the intersection.\n        \"\"\"\n        start1, end1 = interval1\n        start2, end2 = interval2\n\n        # F.1 Process: Determine intersect_start and intersect_end\n        intersect_start = max(start1, start2)\n        intersect_end = min(end1, end2)\n\n        # F.2 Process: Determine intersection validity.\n        if intersect_start > intersect_end:\n            # F.3 Exception: If no valid intersection, length is 0.\n            length = 0\n        else:\n            # F.3 Process: If a valid intersection exists, calculate its length.\n            length = intersect_end - intersect_start + 1\n        \n        return length\ndef intersection(interval1: tuple[int, int], interval2: tuple[int, int]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    The main function for Interval Intersection Prime Length Checker.\n    Calculates the intersection of two closed integer intervals, determines its length,\n    and checks if that length is a prime number.\n    \n    Args:\n        interval1: A tuple (start, end) representing the first closed interval.\n        interval2: A tuple (start, end) representing the second closed interval.\n        \n    Returns:\n        \"YES\" if the intersection length is a prime number, otherwise \"NO\".\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    # F.1, F.2, F.3: Calculate the length of the intersection.\n    intersection_length = IntervalAnalyzer._calculate_intersection_length(interval1, interval2)\n    \n    # F.4: Check for Primality of Length\n    is_length_prime = IntervalAnalyzer._is_prime(intersection_length)\n    \n    # F.5: Return Final Result\n    if is_length_prime:\n        return \"YES\"\n    else:\n        return \"NO\""}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/127", "solution": "import math\ndef _is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a non-negative integer n is a prime number.\n\n    A prime number is a natural number greater than 1 that has no positive divisors\n    other than 1 and itself. Numbers less than or equal to 1 are not considered prime.\n\n    Args:\n        n: The integer to check for primality.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if n is prime, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    if n <= 3:  # 2 and 3 are prime\n        return True\n    if n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:\n        return False\n    \n    # Check for primality using an optimized loop:\n    # All primes greater than 3 can be expressed in the form 6k \u00b1 1.\n    # So we only need to check divisors of the form 6k \u00b1 1.\n    i = 5\n    while i * i <= n:\n        if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 6\n    return True\ndef _get_interval_intersection(interval1: tuple[int, int], interval2: tuple[int, int]) -> tuple[int, int] | None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the intersection of two closed integer intervals.\n\n    Args:\n        interval1: The first closed interval (start1, end1).\n        interval2: The second closed interval (start2, end2).\n\n    Returns:\n        A tuple (start, end) representing the intersected interval, or None if\n        the intervals do not intersect.\n    \"\"\"\n    s1, e1 = interval1\n    s2, e2 = interval2\n\n    start_intersect = max(s1, s2)\n    end_intersect = min(e1, e2)\n\n    if start_intersect > end_intersect:\n        return None  # No intersection\n    else:\n        return (start_intersect, end_intersect)\ndef _get_interval_length(interval: tuple[int, int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the inclusive length of a closed integer interval.\n\n    Args:\n        interval: A tuple (start, end) representing the closed interval.\n\n    Returns:\n        The inclusive length of the interval (end - start + 1).\n    \"\"\"\n    start, end = interval\n    return end - start + 1\ndef intersection(interval1: tuple[int, int], interval2: tuple[int, int]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if the length of the intersection of two given integer intervals\n    is a prime number.\n\n    Args:\n        interval1: A tuple representing the first closed interval, (start1, end1),\n                   where start1 <= end1.\n        interval2: A tuple representing the second closed interval, (start2, end2),\n                   where start2 <= end2.\n\n    Returns:\n        A string literal \"YES\" if the intersection length is prime, \"NO\" otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    intersected_interval = _get_interval_intersection(interval1, interval2)\n\n    if intersected_interval is None:\n        # REQ-FUNC-005: If no intersection, return \"NO\".\n        return \"NO\"\n    else:\n        # REQ-FUNC-003: Calculate the length of the intersection.\n        length = _get_interval_length(intersected_interval)\n        \n        # REQ-FUNC-004: Check primality of the length.\n        is_length_prime = _is_prime(length)\n        \n        if is_length_prime:\n            # REQ-FUNC-006: If the length is prime, return \"YES\".\n            return \"YES\"\n        else:\n            # REQ-FUNC-007: If the length is not prime, return \"NO\".\n            return \"NO\""}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/128", "solution": "def prod_signs(arr: list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates a combined value from an array of integers based on their magnitudes and signs.\n\n    The function computes the sum of the magnitudes (absolute values) of all integers\n    in the input array and multiplies this sum by the product of their signs.\n    The sign of a number is defined as:\n    - 1 for positive numbers\n    - -1 for negative numbers\n    - 0 for zero\n\n    If the input array is empty, the function returns None.\n\n    Args:\n        arr: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the product of the sum of magnitudes and the product of signs.\n        Returns None if the input array `arr` is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return None\n\n    sign_product = 1\n    magnitude_sum = 0\n\n    for num in arr:\n        # Determine the sign of the current number\n        if num > 0:\n            current_sign = 1\n        elif num < 0:\n            current_sign = -1\n        else:\n            current_sign = 0\n\n        # Update the product of signs\n        sign_product *= current_sign\n\n        # Update the sum of magnitudes\n        magnitude_sum += abs(num)\n\n    return magnitude_sum * sign_product"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/128", "solution": "import math\ndef prod_signs(arr: list[int]) -> int | None:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of magnitudes of all integers in an array multiplied by the product of their signs.\n\n    The sign of a positive number is 1, a negative number is -1, and zero is 0.\n    If any number in the array is zero, the overall product of signs becomes zero.\n    An empty input array is handled by returning None.\n\n    Args:\n        arr: A Python list containing integer values.\n\n    Returns:\n        An int: The calculated value (sum of magnitudes multiplied by product of signs).\n        None: If the input arr is an empty list.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return None\n\n    magnitude_sum = 0\n    sign_product = 1\n\n    for x in arr:\n        # Accumulate magnitude sum\n        magnitude_sum += abs(x)\n\n        # Update sign product\n        if x == 0:\n            sign_product = 0\n            break  # If any element is 0, the sign product becomes 0, no need to check further\n        elif x < 0:\n            sign_product *= -1\n        # If x > 0, sign_product effectively multiplies by 1, so no change needed.\n\n    return magnitude_sum * sign_product"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/129", "solution": "import math\nclass GridPathFinder:\n    \"\"\"\n    A class to find the lexicographically smallest path of a specified length k\n    within a given square grid.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, grid: list[list[int]], k: int):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initializes the GridPathFinder with the grid and desired path length.\n\n        Parameters:\n            grid (list[list[int]]): The N x N grid of integer values.\n            k (int): The desired length of the path.\n        \"\"\"\n        self.grid = grid\n        self.N = len(grid)\n        self.k = k\n        # Initialize min_path_values with a list of 'infinity' values.\n        # This ensures that the first valid path found will always be considered smaller.\n        # This list will eventually hold the lexicographically smallest path of length k.\n        self.min_path_values = [math.inf] * k\n        \n        # Define possible movement directions (right, left, down, up)\n        self.directions = [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]\n\n    def _is_valid(self, r: int, c: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Checks if the given coordinates (r, c) are within the grid boundaries.\n\n        Parameters:\n            r (int): The row index.\n            c (int): The column index.\n\n        Returns:\n            bool: True if coordinates are valid, False otherwise.\n        \"\"\"\n        return 0 <= r < self.N and 0 <= c < self.N\n\n    def _dfs(self, r: int, c: int, current_path_values: list[int]) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Performs a Depth-First Search (DFS) to explore paths from a given cell.\n        It incorporates lexicographical comparison and pruning to optimize the search.\n\n        Parameters:\n            r (int): The current row index.\n            c (int): The current column index.\n            current_path_values (list[int]): The list of values in the path\n                                             constructed so far. This list is\n                                             modified in place during recursion.\n        \"\"\"\n        # Add the current cell's value to the path\n        current_path_values.append(self.grid[r][c])\n\n        current_length = len(current_path_values)\n\n        # FR5: Lexicographical Comparison and Pruning\n        # Check if the current partial path is already lexicographically greater\n        # than the corresponding prefix of the currently stored min_path_values.\n        # If so, this branch cannot lead to a smaller path, so we prune it.\n        for i in range(current_length):\n            # If the current path has already exceeded the length of min_path_values\n            # (which is k), or if an element is greater, prune.\n            # We only compare up to the current_length, which can't exceed k.\n            if current_path_values[i] > self.min_path_values[i]:\n                current_path_values.pop() # Backtrack: remove current cell's value\n                return\n            # If an element in the current path is smaller, this path is potentially\n            # better, so we can stop comparing prefixes and continue exploring.\n            elif current_path_values[i] < self.min_path_values[i]:\n                break # This path is smaller so far, continue exploring\n\n        # FR3: Path Length Control - Base case: If path length k is reached\n        if current_length == self.k:\n            # If we reached this point, 'current_path_values' is either\n            # equal to or lexicographically smaller than 'min_path_values'\n            # up to its current length (k) due to the pruning logic above.\n            # We perform a final full comparison to update if 'current_path_values'\n            # is strictly lexicographically smaller (FR5).\n            # Python's built-in list comparison handles lexicographical order naturally.\n            if current_path_values < self.min_path_values:\n                # Update the global minimum path. Make a copy to avoid\n                # modifications by reference from ongoing DFS branches.\n                self.min_path_values = list(current_path_values)\n            current_path_values.pop() # Backtrack: remove current cell's value\n            return\n\n        # FR2: Depth-First Search (DFS) Mechanism - Recursive step: Explore neighbor cells\n        for dr, dc in self.directions:\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n            # FR4: Grid Boundary Enforcement - Ensure neighbor is within grid boundaries\n            # The problem statement implies cells can be revisited within a path,\n            # so no 'visited' set is maintained for the current path.\n            if self._is_valid(nr, nc):\n                self._dfs(nr, nc, current_path_values)\n        \n        # Backtrack: Remove the current cell's value after exploring all branches from it\n        current_path_values.pop()\n\n    def find_min_path(self) -> list[int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Initiates path exploration from every cell in the grid (FR1)\n        and returns the lexicographically smallest path (FR6).\n\n        Returns:\n            list[int]: The values of the cells in the lexicographically\n                       smallest path of length k.\n        \"\"\"\n        # FR1: Path Exploration Initialization - Iterate through every cell as a starting point\n        for r in range(self.N):\n            for c in range(self.N):\n                # Start DFS from each cell with an empty current path\n                self._dfs(r, c, [])\n        \n        # FR6: Return Minimum Path - Return the globally found minimum path\n        return self.min_path_values\ndef minPath(grid: list[list[int]], k: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a grid with N rows and N columns (N >= 2) and a positive integer k, \n    each cell of the grid contains a value. Every integer in the range [1, N * N]\n    inclusive appears exactly once on the cells of the grid.\n\n    You have to find the minimum path of length k in the grid. You can start\n    from any cell, and in each step you can move to any of the neighbor cells,\n    in other words, you can go to cells which share an edge with your current\n    cell.\n    Please note that a path of length k means visiting exactly k cells (not\n    necessarily distinct).\n    You CANNOT go off the grid.\n    A path A (of length k) is considered less than a path B (of length k) if\n    after making the ordered lists of the values on the cells that A and B go\n    through (let's call them lst_A and lst_B), lst_A is lexicographically less\n    than lst_B, in other words, there exists an integer index i (1 <= i <= k)\n    such that lst_A[i] < lst_B[i] and for any j (1 <= j < i) we have\n    lst_A[j] = lst_B[j].\n    It is guaranteed that the answer is unique.\n    Return an ordered list of the values on the cells that the minimum path goes through.\n\n    Examples:\n        Input: grid = [ [1,2,3], [4,5,6], [7,8,9]], k = 3\n        Output: [1, 2, 1]\n\n        Input: grid = [ [5,9,3], [4,1,6], [7,8,2]], k = 1\n        Output: [1]\n    \"\"\"\n    # Create an instance of the GridPathFinder and use it to find the minimum path\n    finder = GridPathFinder(grid, k)\n    return finder.find_min_path()"}
