{"task_id": "HumanEval/130", "solution": "def tri(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates the first n+1 numbers of a specific Tribonacci-like sequence.\n\n    The sequence is defined as:\n    - tri(0) = 1\n    - tri(1) = 3\n    - tri(k) = 1 + k // 2, if k is even and k > 0.\n    - tri(k) = tri(k - 1) + tri(k - 2) + tri(k + 1), if k is odd and k > 1.\n\n    The function handles non-negative integer `n`.\n\n    Args:\n        n: A non-negative integer representing the upper bound of the sequence index.\n           The function returns numbers from index 0 to n.\n\n    Returns:\n        A list of integers representing the first n+1 numbers of the sequence.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is a negative integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input n must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    # F.1.9 Internal Storage Initialization: Initialize a list tribonacci_sequence of size n + 2.\n    # The size n + 2 is needed because calculating tri(k) for odd k requires tri(k + 1).\n    # All elements are initialized to 0; they will be overwritten.\n    tribonacci_sequence = [0] * (n + 2)\n\n    # F.1.5 Base Case tri(0): Set the value for index 0.\n    tribonacci_sequence[0] = 1\n    \n    # F.1.6 Base Case tri(1): Set the value for index 1.\n    # This is valid because tribonacci_sequence has size n+2, so index 1 is always accessible\n    # for n >= 0 (minimum size is 2 for n=0).\n    tribonacci_sequence[1] = 3\n\n    # F.1.10 Population of Even Terms: Populate all even terms in the tribonacci_sequence\n    # up to index n + 1 based on F.1.7.\n    # The rule tri(k) = 1 + k // 2 applies if k is even and k > 0.\n    # We iterate from k=2 (first even k > 0) up to n+1 (inclusive).\n    for k in range(2, n + 2):\n        if k % 2 == 0:  # If k is even\n            tribonacci_sequence[k] = 1 + k // 2\n\n    # F.1.11 Population of Odd Terms: Iterate from i = 3 up to n (inclusive) to calculate\n    # and store odd terms based on F.1.8.\n    # The rule is tri(k) = tri(k - 1) + tri(k - 2) + tri(k + 1), if k is odd and k > 1.\n    # The loop starts from i=3, which covers the 'k > 1' condition for odd k.\n    # The range goes up to n (inclusive), so range(3, n + 1).\n    for i in range(3, n + 1):\n        if i % 2 != 0:  # If i is odd\n            # Ensure i+1 is within bounds. tribonacci_sequence has size n+2,\n            # so the maximum valid index is n+1. If i = n, then i+1 = n+1, which is valid.\n            # tribonacci_sequence[i+1] must be already calculated as it is an even term,\n            # which was handled in the previous loop.\n            tribonacci_sequence[i] = tribonacci_sequence[i - 1] + \\\n                                     tribonacci_sequence[i - 2] + \\\n                                     tribonacci_sequence[i + 1]\n\n    # F.1.12 Final Return Value: Return a slice of the tribonacci_sequence list\n    # containing elements from index 0 to n (inclusive).\n    # This ensures the list length is n + 1 (F.1.4).\n    return tribonacci_sequence[:n+1]"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/130", "solution": "from typing import List\nclass TribonacciSequenceCalculator:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Tribonacci-like sequence numbers based on the defined recurrence relations.\n    This class utilizes memoization to optimize calculations for efficiency.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self):\n        \"\"\"\n        Initializes the TribonacciSequenceCalculator with an empty memoization cache.\n        \"\"\"\n        self._memo = {}\n\n    def _compute_value(self, k: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"\n        Recursively computes the Tribonacci value for a given index k, using memoization.\n        \n        Args:\n            k: The non-negative integer index for which to compute the Tribonacci value.\n        \n        Returns:\n            The integer value of tri(k).\n        \"\"\"\n        if k in self._memo:\n            return self._memo[k]\n\n        if k == 0:\n            value = 1\n        elif k == 1:\n            value = 3\n        elif k % 2 == 0:  # k is even and k > 1\n            value = 1 + k // 2\n        else:  # k is odd and k > 1\n            # tri(k) = tri(k-1) + tri(k-2) + tri(k+1)\n            value = self._compute_value(k - 1) + \\\n                    self._compute_value(k - 2) + \\\n                    self._compute_value(k + 1)\n        \n        self._memo[k] = value\n        return value\n\n    def get_sequence(self, n: int) -> List[int]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Returns a list of the first n+1 numbers of the Tribonacci sequence,\n        from tri(0) up to tri(n).\n        \n        Args:\n            n: A non-negative integer representing the upper bound (inclusive)\n               of the sequence index to calculate.\n        \n        Returns:\n            A list of integers [tri(0), tri(1), ..., tri(n)].\n            \n        Raises:\n            ValueError: If n is a negative integer.\n        \"\"\"\n        if n < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Input n must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n        result = []\n        for i in range(n + 1):\n            result.append(self._compute_value(i))\n        return result\ndef tri(n: int) -> List[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes the first n+1 numbers of the Tribonacci-like sequence.\n    \n    The sequence is defined by the recurrence:\n    tri(0) = 1\n    tri(1) = 3\n    tri(n) = 1 + n / 2, if n is even and n > 1.\n    tri(n) = tri(n - 1) + tri(n - 2) + tri(n + 1), if n is odd and n > 1.\n    \n    Args:\n        n: A non-negative integer.\n        \n    Returns:\n        A list of the first n+1 numbers of the Tribonacci sequence: [tri(0), tri(1), ..., tri(n)].\n        \n    Examples:\n        tri(3) = [1, 3, 2, 8]\n        tri(0) = [1]\n        tri(1) = [1, 3]\n        tri(2) = [1, 3, 2]\n    \"\"\"\n    calculator = TribonacciSequenceCalculator()\n    return calculator.get_sequence(n)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/131", "solution": "def digits(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the product of the odd digits of a positive integer n.\n    Returns 0 if all digits are even.\n\n    Examples:\n        digits(1) == 1\n        digits(4) == 0\n        digits(235) == 15\n        digits(246) == 0\n        digits(12) == 1\n        digits(79) == 63\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): A positive integer. Assumed to be n > 0 as per SRS.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The product of the odd digits, or 0 if all digits are even.\n    \"\"\"\n    product_of_odd_digits = 1\n    found_odd_digit = False\n    current_number = n\n\n    while current_number > 0:\n        digit = current_number % 10  # Get the last digit\n\n        if digit % 2 != 0:  # Check if the digit is odd\n            product_of_odd_digits *= digit\n            found_odd_digit = True\n\n        current_number //= 10  # Remove the last digit\n\n    if found_odd_digit:\n        return product_of_odd_digits\n    else:\n        return 0"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/131", "solution": "def digits(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the product of all odd digits within a given positive integer n.\n    If no odd digits are present in n, the function returns 0.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): A positive integer (n >= 1).\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The product of all odd digits found in n. Returns 0 if n contains no odd digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    product_of_odd_digits = 1\n    found_odd_digit = False\n\n    # Convert the input integer to its string representation to iterate over digits\n    s_n = str(n)\n\n    for char_digit in s_n:\n        current_digit = int(char_digit)\n        if current_digit % 2 != 0:  # Check if the current digit is odd\n            product_of_odd_digits *= current_digit\n            found_odd_digit = True\n\n    if found_odd_digit:\n        return product_of_odd_digits\n    else:\n        return 0"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "def is_nested(string):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a string of square brackets contains a nested pattern `[[]]`.\n\n    Args:\n        string: A string containing only square brackets (`[` and `]`).\n\n    Returns:\n        True if there exists a valid subsequence of brackets where at least\n        one bracket is nested (i.e., `[[]]` subsequence exists); False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    \n    idx1 = -1\n    idx2 = -1\n    idx3 = -1\n    idx4 = -1\n\n    # Step a: Find the index of the first opening bracket (`[`)\n    idx1 = string.find('[')\n    if idx1 == -1:\n        return False\n\n    # Step b: Find the index of the second opening bracket (`[`) after `idx1`\n    idx2 = string.find('[', idx1 + 1)\n    if idx2 == -1:\n        return False\n\n    # Step c: Find the index of the first closing bracket (`]`) after `idx2`\n    idx3 = string.find(']', idx2 + 1)\n    if idx3 == -1:\n        return False\n\n    # Step d: Find the index of the second closing bracket (`]`) after `idx3`\n    idx4 = string.find(']', idx3 + 1)\n    if idx4 == -1:\n        return False\n\n    # Step e: If all four indices are found successfully, return True\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "def is_nested(string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a string containing only square brackets has a valid subsequence\n    with at least one nested bracket.\n\n    A nested bracket subsequence means a pattern like '[[]]' or '[[][]]' exists.\n\n    The function uses a state machine to detect the pattern '[[...]]'.\n\n    States:\n    0: Initial state, no relevant '[' found, or sequence reset by a balanced '[]'.\n       Looking for the first '[' of a potential nested pair.\n    1: Found one '['.\n       Looking for a second '[' to indicate potential nesting, or a ']' for a simple '[]'.\n    2: Found two or more '[' in a sequence (e.g., '[['). This is the key state\n       indicating potential nesting (e.g., the outer brackets of '[[]]').\n       Looking for a ']' to close the first inner pair.\n    3: Found ']' after at least two opening brackets (e.g., '[[ ]'). This ']' closes\n       what could be an inner pair.\n       Looking for a second ']' to complete the nested structure '[[]]'.\n    4: Found ']]' after '[[', implying '[[]]', confirming nesting.\n       Once in this state, nesting is confirmed, and no further processing is needed.\n\n    Args:\n        string: The input string containing only '[' and ']' characters.\n                Assumed to contain only these characters as per constraints.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if a valid nested subsequence is found, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    state = 0  # Initial state\n\n    for char_index in range(len(string)):\n        char = string[char_index]\n\n        if state == 4:\n            # Nesting already confirmed, no need to process further.\n            return True\n\n        if char == '[':\n            if state == 0:\n                state = 1\n            elif state == 1:\n                state = 2  # Transition from '[' to '[['\n            elif state == 2:\n                state = 2  # Already in potential nested state, e.g., '[[['\n            elif state == 3:\n                # Example: '[[ ]['. This ']' essentially closes the potential\n                # inner pair of the *first* '[['. The new '[' then brings\n                # us back to state 2, looking for another inner pair.\n                # It means we are effectively seeing '[[[' structure again.\n                state = 2\n\n        elif char == ']':\n            if state == 0:\n                state = 0  # Unmatched ']', or previous '[]' sequence\n            elif state == 1:\n                state = 0  # Found '[]', not nested, reset state\n            elif state == 2:\n                state = 3  # Found '[[ ]', one ']' for the inner part\n            elif state == 3:\n                state = 4  # Found '[[]]', nesting confirmed!\n    \n    return state == 4"}
