{"task_id": "HumanEval/137", "solution": "from typing import Union, Optional, Tuple\ndef _parse_numeric_input(value: Union[int, float, str]) -> Tuple[float, type]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Helper function to convert an input value to a float for comparison\n    and return its original Python type.\n    Handles string inputs with ',' or '.' as decimal separators.\n\n    Args:\n        value (int | float | str): The input value.\n\n    Returns:\n        Tuple[float, type]: A tuple containing the numeric (float) representation\n                            and the original type of the input value.\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If a string input cannot be parsed as a real number.\n        TypeError: If an unsupported input type is provided.\n    \"\"\"\n    original_type = type(value)\n    if isinstance(value, str):\n        # REQ-FUN-002: Preprocess by replacing comma with period\n        numeric_str = value.replace(',', '.')\n        try:\n            # REQ-FUN-003: Convert to a common internal numeric type (float)\n            return float(numeric_str), original_type\n        except ValueError:\n            # As per requirements, we assume valid number strings.\n            # If invalid strings were allowed, more robust error handling\n            # or returning a default/sentinel would be needed.\n            raise ValueError(f\"Invalid numeric string input: '{value}'\")\n    elif isinstance(value, (int, float)):\n        # REQ-FUN-003: Convert to a common internal numeric type (float)\n        return float(value), original_type\n    else:\n        # According to requirements, inputs are int, float, or str.\n        # This handles unexpected types gracefully.\n        raise TypeError(f\"Unsupported input type: {original_type}. Expected int, float, or str.\")\ndef compare_one(a: Union[int, float, str], b: Union[int, float, str]) -> Optional[Union[int, float, str]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compares two values (integers, floats, or strings representing real numbers)\n    and returns the numerically larger value in its given variable type.\n    Returns None if the values are numerically equal.\n\n    Note: If a real number is represented as a string, the floating point might be '.' or ','.\n\n    Args:\n        a (int | float | str): The first value to compare.\n        b (int | float | str): The second value to compare.\n\n    Returns:\n        (int | float | str | None): The larger value in its original type,\n                                    or None if values are equal.\n\n    Examples:\n        compare_one(1, 2.5) \u279e 2.5\n        compare_one(1, \"2,3\") \u279e \"2,3\"\n        compare_one(\"5,1\", \"6\") \u279e \"6\"\n        compare_one(\"1\", 1) \u279e None\n    \"\"\"\n    # REQ-FUN-001: Accept int, float, or str\n    # Process both inputs to get their numeric values for comparison\n    val_a_numeric, _ = _parse_numeric_input(a)\n    val_b_numeric, _ = _parse_numeric_input(b)\n\n    # REQ-FUN-004: Compare the numeric values\n    if val_a_numeric > val_b_numeric:\n        # REQ-FUN-005: Return the original input 'a'\n        return a\n    elif val_b_numeric > val_a_numeric:\n        # REQ-FUN-006: Return the original input 'b'\n        return b\n    else:  # val_a_numeric == val_b_numeric\n        # REQ-FUN-007: Return None if values are equal\n        return None"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/138", "solution": "# File: even_sum_checker/number_analyzer.py\n\nclass NumberAnalyzer:\n    @staticmethod\n    def is_equal_to_sum_even(n: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Evaluates whether the given number n can be written as the sum of exactly 4 positive even numbers.\n\n        Args:\n            n (int): The integer to evaluate.\n\n        Returns:\n            bool: True if n can be written as the sum of 4 positive even numbers, False otherwise.\n\n        Examples:\n            is_equal_to_sum_even(4) == False\n            is_equal_to_sum_even(6) == False\n            is_equal_to_sum_even(8) == True\n            is_equal_to_sum_even(9) == False\n            is_equal_to_sum_even(10) == True\n        \"\"\"\n        # Constraint 1: The sum of 4 positive even numbers must be at least 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8.\n        if n < 8:\n            return False\n        \n        # Constraint 2: The sum of any even numbers is always an even number.\n        # So, n must be an even number.\n        if n % 2 != 0:\n            return False\n        \n        # If n is an even number and n >= 8, it can always be expressed as the sum of 4 positive even numbers.\n        # For example, if N = n/2, we need k1+k2+k3+k4 = N where ki >= 1.\n        # Since N >= 4 (because n >= 8), we can always satisfy this, e.g., k1=N-3, k2=1, k3=1, k4=1.\n        # This translates to: e1=2(N-3), e2=2, e3=2, e4=2, all positive even numbers.\n        # The sum will be 2(N-3) + 2 + 2 + 2 = 2N - 6 + 6 = 2N = n.\n        # All e_i must be positive. Since N >= 4, N-3 >= 1, so 2(N-3) is a positive even number.\n        return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/138", "solution": "def is_equal_to_sum_even(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given number n can be written as the sum of exactly 4 positive even numbers.\n\n    A positive even number is an integer e such that e > 0 and e % 2 == 0 (e.g., 2, 4, 6, ...).\n    The sum must consist of exactly four such numbers.\n\n    Mathematical Reasoning:\n    Let the four positive even numbers be e1, e2, e3, e4.\n    Since each ei must be a positive even number, the smallest possible value for each ei is 2.\n    Therefore, the minimum sum is 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8.\n    If n < 8, it cannot be a sum of four positive even numbers.\n\n    Also, the sum of any four even numbers (e1 + e2 + e3 + e4) will always be an even number.\n    If n is odd, it cannot be a sum of four positive even numbers.\n\n    If n is even and n >= 8, we can always find such numbers.\n    For example, let k = n / 2. We need to find k1, k2, k3, k4 such that k1 + k2 + k3 + k4 = k,\n    where each ki >= 1 (since ei = 2*ki and ei >= 2).\n    Since n is even and n >= 8, then k = n/2 will be an integer and k >= 4.\n    We can always choose k1=1, k2=1, k3=1. Then k4 = k - 3.\n    Since k >= 4, k - 3 will always be >= 1.\n    So, e1=2, e2=2, e3=2, and e4 = 2*(k-3) = n - 6.\n    Since n >= 8, n-6 >= 2, so e4 is a positive even number.\n    Thus, if n is even and n >= 8, it's always possible.\n\n    Examples:\n    - n = 10: Returns True (e.g., 2 + 2 + 2 + 4 = 10)\n    - n = 8: Returns True (e.g., 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8)\n    - n = 7: Returns False (7 is odd)\n    - n = 6: Returns False (6 is even but less than 8)\n\n    Args:\n        n: An integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if n can be written as the sum of exactly 4 positive even numbers, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Condition 1: The sum of four even numbers must be even.\n    # So, n must be even.\n    if n % 2 != 0:\n        return False\n\n    # Condition 2: The smallest sum of four positive even numbers is 2+2+2+2 = 8.\n    # So, n must be at least 8.\n    if n < 8:\n        return False\n\n    # If both conditions are met, it's always possible.\n    return True"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/139", "solution": "import math\ndef special_factorial(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the special factorial (Brazilian factorial) of a positive integer n.\n    The Brazilian factorial is defined as: n! * (n-1)! * (n-2)! * ... * 1!\n    where n > 0.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): A positive integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        int: The special factorial of n.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is not a positive integer.\n\n    Example:\n        >>> special_factorial(4)\n        288\n        (4! * 3! * 2! * 1! = 24 * 6 * 2 * 1 = 288)\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    total_product = 1\n    # Iterate from 1 up to n (inclusive)\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # Calculate the factorial of the current number i\n        current_factorial = math.factorial(i)\n        # Multiply it with the running total product\n        total_product *= current_factorial\n        \n    return total_product"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/139", "solution": "import math\ndef special_factorial(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the \"Brazilian factorial\" of a given positive integer n.\n    The Brazilian factorial is defined as n! * (n-1)! * (n-2)! * ... * 1!.\n\n    Args:\n        n: A positive integer (n > 0).\n\n    Returns:\n        The computed \"Brazilian factorial\" of n as an integer.\n\n    Raises:\n        ValueError: If n is not an integer or is not a positive integer (n <= 0).\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR-1: Input Validation\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer (n > 0).\")\n\n    # FR-3: Brazilian Factorial Calculation\n    brazilian_factorial_result = 1\n\n    # Iterate from n down to 1\n    for i in range(n, 0, -1):\n        # FR-2: Standard Factorial Calculation\n        # math.factorial(i) efficiently calculates i!\n        current_factorial = math.factorial(i)\n        brazilian_factorial_result *= current_factorial\n\n    # FR-4: Output\n    return brazilian_factorial_result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/140", "solution": "import re\ndef fix_spaces(text: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a string text, replace all spaces in it with underscores,\n    and if a string has more than 2 consecutive spaces,\n    then replace all consecutive spaces with -\n\n    Examples:\n    fix_spaces(\"Example\") == \"Example\"\n    fix_spaces(\"Example 1\") == \"Example_1\"\n    fix_spaces(\" Example 2\") == \"_Example_2\"\n    fix_spaces(\" Example   3\") == \"_Example-3\"\n    fix_spaces(\"A  B   C    D\") == \"A__B-C-D\"\n    \"\"\"\n    # Step 1: Replace sequences of 3 or more consecutive spaces with a single hyphen.\n    # This must be done first to prevent them from being individually replaced by underscores.\n    intermediate_text = re.sub(r\" {3,}\", \"-\", text)\n\n    # Step 2: Replace all remaining single space characters with an underscore.\n    # This handles original single spaces and spaces within original two-space sequences.\n    final_text = re.sub(r\" \", \"_\", intermediate_text)\n\n    return final_text"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/140", "solution": "import re\ndef fix_spaces(text: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Normalizes spaces in a string:\n    1. Replaces two or more consecutive spaces with a single hyphen \"-\".\n    2. Replaces single spaces with an underscore \"_\".\n\n    Args:\n        text (str): The input string.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: The string with spaces fixed according to the rules.\n\n    Examples:\n        fix_spaces(\"Example\")        -> \"Example\"\n        fix_spaces(\"Example 1\")       -> \"Example_1\"\n        fix_spaces(\" Example 2\")      -> \"_Example_2\"\n        fix_spaces(\" Example   3\")     -> \"_Example-3\"\n        fix_spaces(\"text  with   multiple    spaces\") -> \"text-with-multiple-spaces\"\n    \"\"\"\n    # Rule 1: Replace two or more consecutive spaces with a single hyphen \"-\"\n    # This must be done first to avoid single space replacement interfering\n    # with longer sequences.\n    modified_text = re.sub(r' {2,}', '-', text)\n\n    # Rule 2: Replace any remaining single spaces with an underscore \"_\"\n    final_text = re.sub(r' ', '_', modified_text)\n\n    return final_text"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/141", "solution": "def file_name_check(file_name: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates a file name based on specific rules.\n\n    Args:\n        file_name: The string representing the file name to validate.\n\n    Returns:\n        'Yes' if the file name is valid, 'No' otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR2: Dot Count Rule: The file_name shall contain exactly one dot.\n    if file_name.count('.') != 1:\n        return 'No'\n\n    name_part, extension_part = file_name.split('.')\n\n    # FR3: Name Part Not Empty Rule: The part before the dot shall not be empty.\n    if not name_part:\n        return 'No'\n\n    # FR4: Name Part Start Letter Rule: The first character of the part before the dot\n    # shall be an English letter (a-z, A-Z).\n    if not name_part[0].isalpha():\n        return 'No'\n\n    # FR1: Digit Count Rule: The file_name shall contain no more than three digits.\n    digit_count = sum(1 for char in file_name if char.isdigit())\n    if digit_count > 3:\n        return 'No'\n\n    # FR5: Extension Part Validity Rule: The part after the dot shall be one of 'txt', 'exe', or 'dll'.\n    if extension_part not in ['txt', 'exe', 'dll']:\n        return 'No'\n\n    # FR6: Overall Validation Result: Return 'Yes' if all rules are satisfied.\n    return 'Yes'"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/141", "solution": "def file_name_check(file_name: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Validates a file name string based on specific rules.\n\n    Args:\n        file_name (str): The name of the file to be validated.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: 'Yes' if the file name is valid, 'No' otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR2, FR3: Count digits and ensure not more than three\n    digit_count = sum(1 for char in file_name if char.isdigit())\n    if digit_count > 3:\n        return 'No'\n\n    # FR4, FR5: Count dots and ensure exactly one\n    if file_name.count('.') != 1:\n        return 'No'\n\n    # FR6: Split file_name into prefix and suffix\n    # The split method with maxsplit=1 is efficient for this.\n    # We already confirmed there's exactly one dot, so parts will always have 2 elements.\n    prefix, suffix = file_name.split('.', 1)\n\n    # FR7: Ensure prefix is not empty\n    if not prefix:\n        return 'No'\n\n    # FR8: Ensure prefix starts with a letter\n    if not prefix[0].isalpha():\n        return 'No'\n\n    # FR9: Ensure suffix is one of the allowed extensions\n    valid_extensions = {'txt', 'exe', 'dll'}\n    if suffix not in valid_extensions:\n        return 'No'\n\n    # FR10: If all conditions are met, return 'Yes'\n    return 'Yes'"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/142", "solution": "def sum_squares(lst: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of elements in a list after applying conditional transformations.\n\n    For each element at index `i` in the list `lst`:\n    - If `i` is a multiple of 3, the element is squared.\n    - Else if `i` is a multiple of 4, the element is cubed.\n    - Otherwise, the element remains unchanged.\n\n    The function then returns the sum of all processed elements.\n    If the input list is empty, it returns 0.\n\n    Args:\n        lst: A list of integer numbers.\n\n    Returns:\n        An integer representing the sum of the processed list entries.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    for i, value in enumerate(lst):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            total_sum += value ** 2\n        elif i % 4 == 0:\n            total_sum += value ** 3\n        else:\n            total_sum += value\n    return total_sum"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/142", "solution": "def sum_squares(lst):\n    modified_sum = 0\n    for i, num in enumerate(lst):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            modified_sum += num ** 2\n        elif i % 4 == 0:\n            modified_sum += num ** 3\n        else:\n            modified_sum += num\n    return modified_sum"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/143", "solution": "import math\ndef _is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Helper function to determine if a given integer n is a prime number.\n\n    Args:\n        n (int): The integer to check for primality.\n\n    Returns:\n        bool: True if n is prime, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # F3.1: Numbers less than or equal to 1 are not prime.\n    if n <= 1:\n        return False\n    # F3.2: 2 is the only even prime number.\n    if n == 2:\n        return True\n    # Optimization: All other even numbers are not prime.\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        return False\n    # F3.3: Iterate from 3 up to the square root of n (inclusive) to check for odd divisors.\n    # We step by 2 because we've already handled even numbers.\n    for i in range(3, math.isqrt(n) + 1, 2):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\ndef words_in_sentence(sentence: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Filters words from an input sentence, returning a new string composed of\n    words whose lengths are prime numbers.\n\n    Args:\n        sentence (str): The input string representing a sentence. Words are\n                        separated by single spaces and consist only of letters.\n                        Constraints: 1 <= len(sentence) <= 100.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: A new string containing only words whose lengths are prime numbers,\n             maintaining their original order, separated by single spaces.\n             Returns an empty string if no words meet the criteria.\n    \"\"\"\n    # F1. Input Processing: Accept a string `sentence` as input. (handled by function signature)\n    \n    # F2. Word Extraction: Split the input `sentence` into a list of individual words.\n    words = sentence.split(' ')\n    filtered_words = []\n\n    # F4. Word Filtering: Iterate through the extracted words.\n    for word in words:\n        word_length = len(word)\n        # Use the _is_prime function to check if the length is a prime number.\n        if _is_prime(word_length):\n            # F5. Result Aggregation: If a word's length is prime, add it to the list.\n            filtered_words.append(word)\n\n    # F6. Output Generation: Join the words in the filtered list with a single space.\n    # F7. Return Value: Return the resulting string. An empty string is returned if the list is empty.\n    return ' '.join(filtered_words)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/143", "solution": "import math\ndef is_prime(n: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a given integer is a prime number.\n\n    Args:\n        n: An integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if n is prime, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 2:\n        return False\n    if n == 2:\n        return True\n    if n % 2 == 0:  # Optimization: check even numbers explicitly\n        return False\n    # Check for divisibility from 3 up to sqrt(n) (inclusive), only odd numbers\n    for i in range(3, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1, 2):\n        if n % i == 0:\n            return False\n    return True\ndef words_in_sentence(sentence: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Filters words from a sentence, retaining only those whose lengths are prime numbers.\n\n    The order of the filtered words is preserved. Words are separated by single spaces.\n\n    Args:\n        sentence: The input sentence string, expected to contain only letters\n                  (a-z, A-Z) and spaces, with length between 1 and 100 characters.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new string containing only words whose lengths are prime numbers,\n        separated by single spaces. Returns an empty string if no such words exist.\n    \"\"\"\n    words = sentence.split(' ')\n    filtered_words = []\n\n    for word in words:\n        if not word: # Handle potential multiple spaces leading to empty strings if split by ' '\n            continue\n        word_length = len(word)\n        if is_prime(word_length):\n            filtered_words.append(word)\n\n    return \" \".join(filtered_words)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/144", "solution": "from typing import Tuple\ndef parse_fraction(fraction_str: str) -> Tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a fraction string (e.g., \"7/10\") into its numerator and denominator.\n\n    Args:\n        fraction_str: A string representing a fraction in \"numerator/denominator\" format.\n                      Assumed to be valid with positive integer parts and non-zero denominator.\n\n    Returns:\n        A tuple containing two integers: (numerator, denominator).\n    \"\"\"\n    parts = fraction_str.split('/')\n    numerator = int(parts[0])\n    denominator = int(parts[1])\n    return numerator, denominator\ndef simplify(x: str, n: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if the product of two string-represented fractions results in a whole number.\n\n    Args:\n        x: A string representing the first fraction (e.g., \"1/5\").\n           Expected format: \"numerator/denominator\", with positive integer parts.\n        n: A string representing the second fraction (e.g., \"5/1\").\n           Expected format: \"numerator/denominator\", with positive integer parts.\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the product of the two fractions is a whole number, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Parse the first fraction string into its numerator and denominator (FR1, FR2)\n    x_num, x_den = parse_fraction(x)\n\n    # Parse the second fraction string into its numerator and denominator (FR1, FR2)\n    n_num, n_den = parse_fraction(n)\n\n    # Multiply the numerators to get the product numerator (FR3)\n    product_numerator = x_num * n_num\n\n    # Multiply the denominators to get the product denominator (FR4)\n    product_denominator = x_den * n_den\n\n    # Check if the product numerator is perfectly divisible by the product denominator (FR5)\n    # The design assumes denominator will not be zero, so division by zero is not a concern.\n    is_whole_number = (product_numerator % product_denominator == 0)\n\n    # Return the boolean result (FR6)\n    return is_whole_number"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/144", "solution": "def _parse_fraction(fraction_str: str) -> tuple[int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a fraction string of the format '<numerator>/<denominator>'\n    into its corresponding integer numerator and denominator.\n\n    Args:\n        fraction_str: A string representing a fraction (e.g., \"1/2\").\n\n    Returns:\n        A tuple (numerator, denominator) of integers.\n    \"\"\"\n    parts = fraction_str.split('/')\n    numerator = int(parts[0])\n    denominator = int(parts[1])\n    return numerator, denominator\ndef _gcd(a: int, b: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Greatest Common Divisor (GCD) of two non-negative integers\n    using the Euclidean algorithm.\n\n    Args:\n        a: The first integer.\n        b: The second integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        The Greatest Common Divisor of a and b.\n    \"\"\"\n    while b:\n        a, b = b, a % b\n    return a\ndef simplify(x: str, n: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if the product of two fractions (represented as strings)\n    evaluates to a whole number.\n\n    Args:\n        x: A string representing the first fraction (e.g., \"1/2\").\n        n: A string representing the second fraction (e.g., \"4/1\").\n\n    Returns:\n        True if the product of x and n is a whole number, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR-1: Parse Fractions\n    x_num, x_den = _parse_fraction(x)\n    n_num, n_den = _parse_fraction(n)\n\n    # FR-2: Multiply Fractions\n    prod_num = x_num * n_num\n    prod_den = x_den * n_den\n\n    # FR-4 (using FR-3): Simplify Fraction\n    # If numerator or denominator is 0, GCD logic might need special handling.\n    # Given the constraint that numerators/denominators are positive, this is fine.\n    common_divisor = _gcd(prod_num, prod_den)\n\n    simplified_den = prod_den // common_divisor\n\n    # FR-5: Check Whole Number\n    return simplified_den == 1"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/145", "solution": "def _calculate_digit_sum(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of the absolute digits of an integer.\n\n    Args:\n        number: The integer for which to calculate the sum of digits.\n\n    Returns:\n        The sum of the digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Take absolute value to handle negative numbers\n    abs_number = abs(number)\n    \n    # Handle single-digit numbers and zero efficiently\n    if abs_number < 10:\n        return abs_number\n\n    # Convert to string to iterate through digits, then sum them\n    return sum(int(digit) for digit in str(abs_number))\ndef order_by_points(nums: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a list of integers based on the sum of their digits in ascending order.\n    If multiple numbers have the same sum of digits, their relative order is\n    preserved based on their original index in the input list.\n\n    Args:\n        nums: A list of integers.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list of integers sorted according to the specified rules.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not nums:\n        return []\n\n    # Create a list of tuples: (sum_of_digits, original_index, original_number)\n    # This allows Python's built-in sort to handle primary and secondary keys naturally.\n    processed_numbers = []\n    for index, num in enumerate(nums):\n        digit_sum = _calculate_digit_sum(num)\n        processed_numbers.append((digit_sum, index, num))\n\n    # Sort the list of tuples.\n    # Python's default tuple sorting will sort by the first element, then the second, etc.\n    # This naturally satisfies the requirement for primary and secondary sorting keys.\n    processed_numbers.sort()\n\n    # Extract the original numbers from the sorted tuples\n    sorted_nums = [item[2] for item in processed_numbers]\n\n    return sorted_nums"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/145", "solution": "def sum_digits(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of the absolute value of digits of a given integer.\n\n    Args:\n        n: An integer.\n\n    Returns:\n        The sum of the absolute value of its digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    s = 0\n    # Work with the absolute value to handle negative numbers consistently\n    # (e.g., sum of digits for -123 is 1+2+3=6)\n    n = abs(n)\n    while n > 0:\n        s += n % 10\n        n //= 10\n    return s\ndef order_by_points(nums: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sorts a list of integers based on the sum of their digits.\n    In cases where numbers have the same sum of digits, their original\n    relative order in the input list is maintained.\n\n    Args:\n        nums: A list of integers to be sorted.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new list containing the integers sorted according to the specified rules.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not nums:\n        return []\n\n    # Create a temporary list of tuples (number, original_index)\n    # This preserves the original order for tie-breaking during sorting.\n    indexed_nums = [(num, i) for i, num in enumerate(nums)]\n\n    # Use Python's built-in sorted() function for stable sorting.\n    # The key function will calculate the digit sum and use the original index\n    # for secondary sorting (which ensures stability for equal digit sums).\\n    # Python's tuple comparison sorts by the first element, then the second, etc.\n    sorted_indexed_nums = sorted(indexed_nums, key=lambda item: (sum_digits(item[0]), item[1]))\n\n    # Extract just the numbers from the sorted list of tuples\n    result = [item[0] for item in sorted_indexed_nums]\n\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/146", "solution": "def specialFilter(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Filters a list of integers to count 'special numbers'.\n\n    A number is considered 'special' if it meets all the following criteria:\n    1. It is strictly greater than 10.\n    2. Its first digit (considering its absolute value) is an odd number (1, 3, 5, 7, 9).\n    3. Its last digit (considering its absolute value) is an odd number (1, 3, 5, 7, 9).\n\n    Args:\n        nums: A list of integers to be filtered.\n\n    Returns:\n        The total count of special numbers found in the input list.\n    \"\"\"\n    special_count = 0  # FR-SF-002: Initialize a counter for special numbers.\n\n    # FR-SF-003: For each number in the nums list, evaluate it against criteria.\n    for num in nums:\n        # FR-SF-003.1: The number must be strictly greater than 10.\n        if num > 10:\n            # Get the absolute value of the number to ensure consistent digit extraction\n            # for both positive and negative inputs, as per FR-SF-003.2 and FR-SF-003.3.\n            abs_num_str = str(abs(num))\n\n            # Extract the first digit (converted to int)\n            first_digit = int(abs_num_str[0])\n            # Extract the last digit (converted to int)\n            last_digit = int(abs_num_str[-1])\n\n            # FR-SF-003.2: Check if the first digit is odd.\n            is_first_digit_odd = first_digit % 2 != 0\n            # FR-SF-003.3: Check if the last digit is odd.\n            is_last_digit_odd = last_digit % 2 != 0\n\n            # FR-SF-004: If a number satisfies all criteria, increment the counter.\n            if is_first_digit_odd and is_last_digit_odd:\n                special_count += 1\n\n    # FR-SF-005: After processing all numbers, return the final value of the counter.\n    return special_count"}
